WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.510
&gt;&gt; All right, it's
my great pleasure

00:00:07.511 --> 00:00:11.620
to have Geoff Voelker
here, professor at UCSD.

00:00:11.621 --> 00:00:14.295
I think everyone
knows who he is.

00:00:14.296 --> 00:00:17.710
He's done an immense amount

00:00:17.711 --> 00:00:21.110
of impactful work in
the area of security.

00:00:21.111 --> 00:00:23.300
Very recently, he was

00:00:23.301 --> 00:00:25.910
awarded The Test of Time
Award at USENIX security.

00:00:25.911 --> 00:00:29.350
And also, last year he
became an ACM fellow.

00:00:29.351 --> 00:00:32.035
Geoff has had a long history

00:00:32.036 --> 00:00:34.310
with us at Microsoft,
he's a friend of ours.

00:00:34.311 --> 00:00:38.960
He was the first intern in
the Systems Group here at MSR.

00:00:38.961 --> 00:00:42.620
Since then, he has had a lot
of interaction with us,

00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:46.130
both directly and also
indirectly through his students,

00:00:46.131 --> 00:00:48.315
who have come and spent
a lot of time with us.

00:00:48.316 --> 00:00:50.010
And Geoff has also
spent almost an

00:00:50.011 --> 00:00:52.645
entire year in MSR India.

00:00:52.646 --> 00:00:54.840
So, it's a great pleasure

00:00:54.841 --> 00:00:56.130
to have our friend
here. Thank you.

00:00:56.131 --> 00:00:58.120
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.
Thank you, Charlie.

00:00:58.121 --> 00:01:02.325
All right, are you guys
hearing through the mic?

00:01:02.326 --> 00:01:05.345
Great. It's
fantastic to be here.

00:01:05.346 --> 00:01:08.650
It's always fun to
come back to MSR.

00:01:08.651 --> 00:01:11.610
I have many, many great
memories going all the way

00:01:11.611 --> 00:01:13.020
back to when I was an intern and

00:01:13.021 --> 00:01:14.900
when everything was just
getting started here.

00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:18.130
And even when I was
a super green professor,

00:01:18.131 --> 00:01:19.620
just starting at UCSD.

00:01:19.621 --> 00:01:22.610
Victor helped me out
a lot with both support,

00:01:22.611 --> 00:01:23.830
and advice, and mentorship.

00:01:23.831 --> 00:01:25.325
So, I have

00:01:25.326 --> 00:01:28.865
a very close and personal
history with Microsoft.

00:01:28.866 --> 00:01:33.785
What I want to do
today is talk about

00:01:33.786 --> 00:01:36.590
some relatively recent work
that we've been

00:01:36.591 --> 00:01:40.150
doing in sort of this
cybercrime space.

00:01:40.151 --> 00:01:42.290
We call it
Evidence-Based Security.

00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:47.020
And we call this Center

00:01:47.021 --> 00:01:50.900
because the NSF style
funding, that it is.

00:01:50.901 --> 00:01:53.530
But it's essentially
a large grant from

00:01:53.531 --> 00:01:56.355
NSF to do
cybercrime-related research.

00:01:56.356 --> 00:01:58.290
And this is something that we

00:01:58.291 --> 00:02:02.125
also work very closely with
Vern Paxson at Berkeley,

00:02:02.126 --> 00:02:06.360
going all the way back to
way on, in previous projects.

00:02:06.361 --> 00:02:13.560
And, the style of

00:02:13.561 --> 00:02:16.840
security work that we
wound up doing over time,

00:02:16.841 --> 00:02:20.600
sort of, evolved into this style
where, roughly speaking,

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:23.140
we do a tremendous amount
of measurement to be

00:02:23.141 --> 00:02:25.540
able to understand the problem,

00:02:25.541 --> 00:02:27.770
the attackers,
what they're doing,

00:02:27.771 --> 00:02:29.490
how they're impacting users or

00:02:29.491 --> 00:02:31.480
infrastructure, or whatever
is it they're doing.

00:02:31.481 --> 00:02:36.570
And then, when thinking about
how to both understand and

00:02:36.571 --> 00:02:38.210
potentially undermine and deal

00:02:38.211 --> 00:02:41.915
with some style of abuse
or large-scale attack,

00:02:41.916 --> 00:02:43.345
to also not just think about

00:02:43.346 --> 00:02:45.000
technical approaches
but also sort of

00:02:45.001 --> 00:02:48.620
the economics and
social aspects of the attack,

00:02:48.621 --> 00:02:50.420
as well, as additional leverage

00:02:50.421 --> 00:02:52.210
for trying to impact
what's going on.

00:02:52.211 --> 00:02:53.740
And then, finally to

00:02:53.741 --> 00:02:56.420
engage with attackers
where possible.

00:02:56.421 --> 00:03:00.225
And so, in the set of
work that we've done,

00:03:00.226 --> 00:03:02.020
the stuff that's
probably most well-known,

00:03:02.021 --> 00:03:03.280
that we've given
lot to talk about,

00:03:03.281 --> 00:03:05.270
is all this spam work,

00:03:05.271 --> 00:03:09.250
where we are going after
email spam and eventually search

00:03:09.251 --> 00:03:10.870
spam and we bought
a lot of drugs

00:03:10.871 --> 00:03:13.255
for science and
all this crazy stuff.

00:03:13.256 --> 00:03:15.120
But in reality, actually,

00:03:15.121 --> 00:03:17.520
we've done lots of
different things over time.

00:03:17.521 --> 00:03:22.025
Many, many different kinds
of cybercrime-related work.

00:03:22.026 --> 00:03:25.210
The stuff that I highlighted
in blue are actually

00:03:25.211 --> 00:03:28.425
projects that we worked
together with Microsoft.

00:03:28.426 --> 00:03:30.420
We have a number of
projects that we've done

00:03:30.421 --> 00:03:32.690
with PsyCAT in particular.

00:03:32.691 --> 00:03:35.305
But also the Digital Crime Unit,

00:03:35.306 --> 00:03:37.075
when we were doing all of that,

00:03:37.076 --> 00:03:39.445
that email spam,
and stuff like that.

00:03:39.446 --> 00:03:41.440
So, it's been great working

00:03:41.441 --> 00:03:45.045
with Microsoft on this style
of projects as well.

00:03:45.046 --> 00:03:49.140
So, for today, I
wanted to cover sort of

00:03:49.141 --> 00:03:53.065
two relatively recent projects.

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:55.665
They're not necessarily
tied together directly,

00:03:55.666 --> 00:03:57.990
except that they are
relatively recent.

00:03:57.991 --> 00:04:00.470
One is on something
that we call Tripwire,

00:04:00.471 --> 00:04:02.890
which is sort of the mechanism
for being able to infer

00:04:02.891 --> 00:04:06.175
site compromise externally
as a third party.

00:04:06.176 --> 00:04:08.840
And then, the second
is an effort

00:04:08.841 --> 00:04:12.090
looking at the main
motivation is to,

00:04:12.091 --> 00:04:15.805
sort of, foundations for
looking at abuse related to DNS.

00:04:15.806 --> 00:04:19.520
But starting out by exploring
what is happening with

00:04:19.521 --> 00:04:21.110
the introduction of all of

00:04:21.111 --> 00:04:24.075
these new top-level
domains in DNS.

00:04:24.076 --> 00:04:26.620
And, if there's additional time,

00:04:26.621 --> 00:04:28.935
probably not because I stuck
a lot of slides in here,

00:04:28.936 --> 00:04:31.600
I can talk about where
we're going from here.

00:04:31.601 --> 00:04:34.330
And at any point, please

00:04:34.331 --> 00:04:36.255
feel free to stop me
if you have questions.

00:04:36.256 --> 00:04:38.380
It's much more fun for me if

00:04:38.381 --> 00:04:41.090
it's an interactive style talk.

00:04:41.091 --> 00:04:43.340
Okay. So, I'll start with

00:04:43.341 --> 00:04:44.890
the first project which was

00:04:44.891 --> 00:04:46.520
led by our student Joe DeBlasio.

00:04:46.521 --> 00:04:49.140
We have this system called

00:04:49.141 --> 00:04:52.855
Tripwire for being able
to infer site compromise.

00:04:52.856 --> 00:04:55.350
And, data breaches and

00:04:55.351 --> 00:04:58.775
site compromises are
all over the news.

00:04:58.776 --> 00:05:02.280
Everybody's been compromised
and I can absolutely

00:05:02.281 --> 00:05:03.560
guarantee you the UCSD has

00:05:03.561 --> 00:05:05.955
been compromised
many times over.

00:05:05.956 --> 00:05:10.315
And, one question is,

00:05:10.316 --> 00:05:13.540
how many compromises
are there out there?

00:05:13.541 --> 00:05:16.160
And what is the rate?
Who is being compromised?

00:05:16.161 --> 00:05:19.525
Potentially why?
All these interesting questions.

00:05:19.526 --> 00:05:22.390
The challenge is trying to
measure this stuff externally,

00:05:22.391 --> 00:05:23.440
because nobody wants to admit

00:05:23.441 --> 00:05:24.730
that they've been compromised.

00:05:24.731 --> 00:05:26.810
To me, it's analogous,

00:05:26.811 --> 00:05:29.280
we did this very early
work back in 2001,

00:05:29.281 --> 00:05:31.245
looking at denial
of service attacks.

00:05:31.246 --> 00:05:32.320
Nobody wants to
admit they're being

00:05:32.321 --> 00:05:33.470
attacked but can
you come up with

00:05:33.471 --> 00:05:36.580
ways to infer this externally.

00:05:36.581 --> 00:05:39.910
So, the idea that
we had was that,

00:05:39.911 --> 00:05:45.085
we can get insight into
compromises at sites,

00:05:45.086 --> 00:05:49.855
through the fact that people
re-use their passwords.

00:05:49.856 --> 00:05:52.460
Okay. So in general,

00:05:52.461 --> 00:05:56.160
the idea is that for
any arbitrary site out there,

00:05:56.161 --> 00:05:58.525
let's say it's
even some hobby site,

00:05:58.526 --> 00:05:59.860
users will sign up,

00:05:59.861 --> 00:06:02.945
the site will ask the users
for their email address,

00:06:02.946 --> 00:06:04.550
and then a password,
and then maybe

00:06:04.551 --> 00:06:07.255
the credentials that they'll
use to log in to the site.

00:06:07.256 --> 00:06:11.020
And the reason why

00:06:11.021 --> 00:06:14.140
attackers might break into
a site, compromise it,

00:06:14.141 --> 00:06:17.420
and then use the email account
and the password,

00:06:17.421 --> 00:06:18.970
why it might have value to them,

00:06:18.971 --> 00:06:21.650
is because, if there
is password re-use,

00:06:21.651 --> 00:06:23.200
then they can go and try to log

00:06:23.201 --> 00:06:25.320
in to the email account
of that user.

00:06:25.321 --> 00:06:28.000
So, they breached
some other site over there,

00:06:28.001 --> 00:06:31.580
which then led some leapfrog
into the Webmail account,

00:06:31.581 --> 00:06:34.330
and then they have
all of these assets

00:06:34.331 --> 00:06:35.930
associated with
that email account that they

00:06:35.931 --> 00:06:37.720
can mine and try to monetize.

00:06:37.721 --> 00:06:39.540
So there's actually
a lot of value.

00:06:39.541 --> 00:06:45.080
So, when there are slight
compromises and data breaches,

00:06:45.081 --> 00:06:46.495
particularly of the credentials,

00:06:46.496 --> 00:06:49.290
attackers will routinely
just take all of

00:06:49.291 --> 00:06:52.550
the the email accounts that
they find and the passwords,

00:06:52.551 --> 00:06:53.590
and just try to see if they

00:06:53.591 --> 00:06:54.860
can log into the email account.

00:06:54.861 --> 00:06:57.155
And if so, they
will use it directly

00:06:57.156 --> 00:06:58.850
or resell those email accounts

00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:00.345
in bulk to other attackers.

00:07:00.346 --> 00:07:01.290
There's a lot of value in

00:07:01.291 --> 00:07:02.820
attackers being able to do this.

00:07:02.821 --> 00:07:05.805
Okay, so then,
this sort of suggests,

00:07:05.806 --> 00:07:08.090
a technique for being able to

00:07:08.091 --> 00:07:11.285
detect site compromise
externally.

00:07:11.286 --> 00:07:13.560
So, what I'll do is
talk about, sort of,

00:07:13.561 --> 00:07:15.890
the methodology that we used,

00:07:15.891 --> 00:07:17.310
and then a study that we did

00:07:17.311 --> 00:07:19.170
over the span of
about a year-and-a-half,

00:07:19.171 --> 00:07:22.515
talk about the compromises
that we detected.

00:07:22.516 --> 00:07:24.480
And then, we did
an additional step,

00:07:24.481 --> 00:07:26.380
where we contacted all the sites

00:07:26.381 --> 00:07:27.885
where we detected compromise,

00:07:27.886 --> 00:07:29.250
and sort of interacted with

00:07:29.251 --> 00:07:31.090
them about what it
is that they found.

00:07:31.091 --> 00:07:32.940
And it was actually pretty

00:07:32.941 --> 00:07:35.025
eye-opening
that disclosure step.

00:07:35.026 --> 00:07:36.520
Wasn't quite sure what
was going to happen.

00:07:36.521 --> 00:07:38.900
&gt;&gt; Isn't that like when
you create a honey account?

00:07:38.901 --> 00:07:40.840
&gt;&gt; Exactly, great.

00:07:40.841 --> 00:07:43.785
Okay. So with this,

00:07:43.786 --> 00:07:46.080
the idea is actually
very straightforward.

00:07:46.081 --> 00:07:47.100
We're just going to partner with

00:07:47.101 --> 00:07:48.325
a large Webmail provider,

00:07:48.326 --> 00:07:49.920
who asked to remain anonymous.

00:07:49.921 --> 00:07:51.650
And we're just going
to create a whole bunch

00:07:51.651 --> 00:07:53.725
of honey email accounts.

00:07:53.726 --> 00:07:56.805
And we're going to use
those email accounts

00:07:56.806 --> 00:08:01.085
one-to-one with sites
out on the internet.

00:08:01.086 --> 00:08:06.800
And then, we'll just wait.
Just wait, wait, wait.

00:08:06.801 --> 00:08:09.800
Eventually, an attacker will
compromise one of the sites.

00:08:09.801 --> 00:08:11.850
We don't know which
one, just going to wait.

00:08:11.851 --> 00:08:13.820
And then, they'll try to log

00:08:13.821 --> 00:08:16.335
in to an account at
the email provider.

00:08:16.336 --> 00:08:18.430
And then, the email
provider is going to

00:08:18.431 --> 00:08:20.015
be monitoring
all these accounts,

00:08:20.016 --> 00:08:22.505
and will notify us if there
are any log in attempts,

00:08:22.506 --> 00:08:24.435
whether they're
successful or not.

00:08:24.436 --> 00:08:26.690
And then, potentially
even monitor

00:08:26.691 --> 00:08:27.890
the activity of what

00:08:27.891 --> 00:08:30.900
happens to those accounts
when somebody logs in.

00:08:30.901 --> 00:08:34.980
Okay? That's the general idea.
Very straightforward.

00:08:34.981 --> 00:08:36.540
&gt;&gt; Do you make the passwords

00:08:36.541 --> 00:08:43.065
especially easy or something
to ensure that [inaudible].

00:08:43.066 --> 00:08:45.800
&gt;&gt; Great question
and I'll give you $5.

00:08:45.801 --> 00:08:49.280
Okay. So, when we registered
accounts of the sites,

00:08:49.281 --> 00:08:51.180
we actually registered
two accounts.

00:08:51.181 --> 00:08:53.560
One account that
had an easy to guess

00:08:53.561 --> 00:08:55.760
password and another account

00:08:55.761 --> 00:08:57.550
that had a hard
to crack password.

00:08:57.551 --> 00:08:59.770
And then, when we looked to

00:08:59.771 --> 00:09:02.410
see if there were log in
attempts to our accounts,

00:09:02.411 --> 00:09:03.645
we looked to see whether or not

00:09:03.646 --> 00:09:05.050
the attackers were
able to get in

00:09:05.051 --> 00:09:08.210
to the easy account and
get in to the hard account.

00:09:08.211 --> 00:09:10.475
And if they are able to
get into the hard account,

00:09:10.476 --> 00:09:11.980
then that means that site did

00:09:11.981 --> 00:09:14.520
not do a good job of
securing passwords.

00:09:14.521 --> 00:09:17.600
They're either in the clear
or very easily reverse

00:09:17.601 --> 00:09:19.210
engineered using rainbow tables

00:09:19.211 --> 00:09:21.545
or any standard
method out there.

00:09:21.546 --> 00:09:24.090
So, it lets us
differentiate and get

00:09:24.091 --> 00:09:25.240
some insight into sort of

00:09:25.241 --> 00:09:27.660
the security practices of
the site. Great question.

00:09:27.661 --> 00:09:32.400
Exactly. Doing this for

00:09:32.401 --> 00:09:34.140
one or two sites,
very straightforward.

00:09:34.141 --> 00:09:37.110
So, one of the technical
challenges was scaling this up,

00:09:37.111 --> 00:09:39.790
so that we could just go
down lots of different sites

00:09:39.791 --> 00:09:42.435
and try to register
accounts with them.

00:09:42.436 --> 00:09:44.120
So, a lot of the work

00:09:44.121 --> 00:09:45.910
that Joe had to do
was sort of building

00:09:45.911 --> 00:09:47.530
an automated crawler to be able

00:09:47.531 --> 00:09:50.290
to identify an account
registration page,

00:09:50.291 --> 00:09:53.400
parse everything, and then
sign up for the account,

00:09:53.401 --> 00:09:56.180
get the email message to
confirm the registration,

00:09:56.181 --> 00:10:00.120
click on that, and all
that kind of stuff.

00:10:00.121 --> 00:10:01.680
The pages tend to be written in

00:10:01.681 --> 00:10:03.870
such a way to make it
hard to automate that,

00:10:03.871 --> 00:10:06.130
because in a way,

00:10:06.131 --> 00:10:08.430
large scale account
sign up is not

00:10:08.431 --> 00:10:09.470
necessarily something that we

00:10:09.471 --> 00:10:11.310
want to encourage or make easy.

00:10:11.311 --> 00:10:16.840
And then, we also supplemented
the automated crawler,

00:10:16.841 --> 00:10:18.640
which is going down to sites at

00:10:18.641 --> 00:10:20.850
the top Alexa and

00:10:20.851 --> 00:10:25.040
just doing manual registrations
if the crawler failed.

00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:29.375
And then, the Webmail provider,

00:10:29.376 --> 00:10:34.485
we generated the profiles
for all of the accounts,

00:10:34.486 --> 00:10:38.080
the passwords, and
the account usernames.

00:10:38.081 --> 00:10:39.060
And then, we would just give

00:10:39.061 --> 00:10:40.555
that information to
the Webmail provider,

00:10:40.556 --> 00:10:43.695
and then they would just
create accounts for us in bulk.

00:10:43.696 --> 00:10:46.750
The Webmail provider did not
know which accounts we had

00:10:46.751 --> 00:10:49.780
used or even which

00:10:49.781 --> 00:10:52.395
sites those accounts
were associated with.

00:10:52.396 --> 00:10:53.900
And what was nice about that,

00:10:53.901 --> 00:10:55.490
is that, in the end,

00:10:55.491 --> 00:10:58.445
we had about 100,000

00:10:58.446 --> 00:10:59.610
of these honeypots accounts

00:10:59.611 --> 00:11:01.480
that we didn't sign up anywhere.

00:11:01.481 --> 00:11:04.160
And they were never accessed,

00:11:04.161 --> 00:11:06.690
which gave us
a pretty good indication

00:11:06.691 --> 00:11:08.765
that the Webmail provider
was not breached.

00:11:08.766 --> 00:11:11.860
So, having our accounts
logged in to,

00:11:11.861 --> 00:11:13.660
was not a result of
the Webmail provider

00:11:13.661 --> 00:11:16.855
itself actually
getting breached.

00:11:16.856 --> 00:11:23.115
Okay. So then, we
have this crawler,

00:11:23.116 --> 00:11:26.520
we just go down
the Alexa rankings,

00:11:26.521 --> 00:11:29.970
trying to identify sites
that we can register at.

00:11:29.971 --> 00:11:35.180
For the sites that actually
had registration pages,

00:11:35.181 --> 00:11:38.380
the automated crawler
succeeded reasonably well,

00:11:38.381 --> 00:11:40.610
but it was focused
on English sites,

00:11:40.611 --> 00:11:42.645
there are actually a lot
of non-English Sites

00:11:42.646 --> 00:11:45.660
in the top Alexa.

00:11:45.661 --> 00:11:50.375
And then, some sites that
were very difficult to do,

00:11:50.376 --> 00:11:51.850
just using an automated crawler.

00:11:51.851 --> 00:11:55.250
&gt;&gt; So, of those sites you said,

00:11:55.251 --> 00:11:56.960
you succeeded about 20
percent of the time.

00:11:56.961 --> 00:12:01.810
When you failed,
was it credit card?

00:12:01.811 --> 00:12:03.300
Page part of credit card, or?

00:12:03.301 --> 00:12:05.410
&gt;&gt; It was all sorts
of different things.

00:12:05.411 --> 00:12:09.485
So, they might ask
for a credit card.

00:12:09.486 --> 00:12:12.450
It might be a very
advanced capture where,

00:12:12.451 --> 00:12:14.230
so to solve captures
we would just

00:12:14.231 --> 00:12:16.335
enlist a capture solving
service and pay them.

00:12:16.336 --> 00:12:18.400
And some of those
just couldn't handle,

00:12:18.401 --> 00:12:20.100
the interface of using
the service couldn't

00:12:20.101 --> 00:12:21.970
handle the advanced captures.

00:12:21.971 --> 00:12:25.330
Sometimes sites will ask

00:12:25.331 --> 00:12:27.530
for one bit of
information on one page.

00:12:27.531 --> 00:12:29.560
Make you click, go to
another page, enter,

00:12:29.561 --> 00:12:30.950
and the next bit
information, and so on,

00:12:30.951 --> 00:12:33.635
and so forth, and
so this multi-step.

00:12:33.636 --> 00:12:35.960
There are sites out there,

00:12:35.961 --> 00:12:46.090
so for example, let's say
for power utility sites.

00:12:46.091 --> 00:12:48.235
There's an account there,
but the only way in which you

00:12:48.236 --> 00:12:50.210
can actually login and register,

00:12:50.211 --> 00:12:51.270
is if you actually
have some kind of

00:12:51.271 --> 00:12:53.600
relationship with them already,

00:12:53.601 --> 00:12:55.900
and you can't just arbitrarily.

00:12:55.901 --> 00:12:58.400
So, the bottom line

00:12:58.401 --> 00:12:59.995
is there are lots of
different reasons,

00:12:59.996 --> 00:13:02.310
why the automated
crawler would fail.

00:13:02.311 --> 00:13:05.585
&gt;&gt; The power companies
are in an eligible case?

00:13:05.586 --> 00:13:09.310
&gt;&gt; It would be
an eligible case, yeah.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:13.310
So in the end, we were
able to successfully

00:13:13.311 --> 00:13:17.780
register on 2,300 sites,

00:13:17.781 --> 00:13:18.810
which was less than

00:13:18.811 --> 00:13:20.110
we thought we were
going to be able to

00:13:20.111 --> 00:13:23.080
do going into the project but,

00:13:23.081 --> 00:13:27.350
still sort of, is a reasonably
sized number. And then-

00:13:27.351 --> 00:13:29.740
&gt;&gt; If you studied this over,

00:13:29.741 --> 00:13:33.450
if you would study this
over a period of time,

00:13:33.451 --> 00:13:35.500
would you start to see that it's

00:13:35.501 --> 00:13:38.235
getting harder or easier,

00:13:38.236 --> 00:13:41.680
I would hope harder, to
create such accounts?

00:13:41.681 --> 00:13:42.390
&gt;&gt; I think it's harder.

00:13:42.391 --> 00:13:44.780
&gt;&gt; So the number 23,
like for example,

00:13:44.781 --> 00:13:46.790
if you started to study
again for another two years,

00:13:46.791 --> 00:13:49.475
it should come down too,
which is a good thing. Right?

00:13:49.476 --> 00:13:51.165
&gt;&gt; It is a good thing,
but at the same time,

00:13:51.166 --> 00:13:54.010
if we invested more effort
in our crawler,

00:13:54.011 --> 00:13:57.480
then we can sort
of take no account,

00:13:57.481 --> 00:14:00.300
and deal with
more sophisticated ways

00:14:00.301 --> 00:14:01.695
of registering accounts.

00:14:01.696 --> 00:14:05.020
So I think, we could
still sort of maintain it.

00:14:05.021 --> 00:14:08.625
&gt;&gt; I guess my point was that,

00:14:08.626 --> 00:14:11.920
so I'm sort of thinking like
long time back you might

00:14:11.921 --> 00:14:12.975
remember when we were sort of

00:14:12.976 --> 00:14:14.660
thinking about Wi-Fi, right?

00:14:14.661 --> 00:14:17.470
Where everything was
accessible, but then,

00:14:17.471 --> 00:14:18.545
everything came with default

00:14:18.546 --> 00:14:20.265
passwords, it became difficult.

00:14:20.266 --> 00:14:21.740
But then everything
was, you know, so

00:14:21.741 --> 00:14:23.160
eventually, like it
kept going downward.

00:14:23.161 --> 00:14:26.000
And so then, compromising
it became harder.

00:14:26.001 --> 00:14:27.620
And so there was a lot
of papers written

00:14:27.621 --> 00:14:28.970
in the past saying,

00:14:28.971 --> 00:14:30.170
"Hey, you can
connect, and you can

00:14:30.171 --> 00:14:31.770
do all this thing. But now."

00:14:31.771 --> 00:14:33.030
&gt;&gt; Now it's not even an issue.

00:14:33.031 --> 00:14:34.230
&gt;&gt; All that stuff is just gone.

00:14:34.231 --> 00:14:36.200
&gt;&gt; I don't think this
is going to go away,

00:14:36.201 --> 00:14:40.205
because the sort of
the counterbalance is that

00:14:40.206 --> 00:14:42.465
sites in general
don't want to make it

00:14:42.466 --> 00:14:44.790
too hard for people
to register accounts.

00:14:44.791 --> 00:14:45.920
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:14:45.921 --> 00:14:47.860
&gt;&gt; Particularly if
you're not a top site.

00:14:47.861 --> 00:14:49.770
So you don't want
to make it hard and

00:14:49.771 --> 00:14:51.975
push people away because
you want to attract people.

00:14:51.976 --> 00:14:57.575
So that's why I think
it's going to balance out.

00:14:57.576 --> 00:15:00.890
The end result of doing
the monitor is that we detected

00:15:00.891 --> 00:15:02.325
19 site breaches which is

00:15:02.326 --> 00:15:06.055
almost one percent of the sites
that we are monitoring,

00:15:06.056 --> 00:15:08.890
which is actually more

00:15:08.891 --> 00:15:11.765
than I thought we
were going to detect.

00:15:11.766 --> 00:15:15.540
So now I'll talk
a little bit about, sort of,

00:15:15.541 --> 00:15:17.050
characteristics of the sites

00:15:17.051 --> 00:15:19.230
that we did detect
the breaches at.

00:15:19.231 --> 00:15:23.390
So these are tables, we
just letter the sites,

00:15:23.391 --> 00:15:28.045
A through S. The check mark
in the hard column means

00:15:28.046 --> 00:15:31.890
that the honeypot account

00:15:31.891 --> 00:15:33.200
that we had associated
with the site,

00:15:33.201 --> 00:15:36.270
the attacker was able to
login using the hard password.

00:15:36.271 --> 00:15:40.690
So that meant, you know,
bad practices on the site.

00:15:40.691 --> 00:15:43.390
This is the yield,
the Alexa ranking,

00:15:43.391 --> 00:15:45.460
sort of modular 500.

00:15:45.461 --> 00:15:47.790
And then sort of the category of

00:15:47.791 --> 00:15:51.970
the style of site that we see.

00:15:51.971 --> 00:15:55.555
One nice little bit of
validation was that,

00:15:55.556 --> 00:15:56.900
for all of these sites,

00:15:56.901 --> 00:16:00.070
they had not been
confirmed to ever have been

00:16:00.071 --> 00:16:04.325
compromised except
for bitcointalk.org.

00:16:04.326 --> 00:16:07.205
They went public about
being compromised,

00:16:07.206 --> 00:16:09.010
and we had already had
a honeypot account.

00:16:09.011 --> 00:16:10.320
And we were able to confirm that

00:16:10.321 --> 00:16:12.080
exactly when they said
they were compromised,

00:16:12.081 --> 00:16:13.930
sure enough the next day,

00:16:13.931 --> 00:16:16.225
somebody logged into
our e-mail account.

00:16:16.226 --> 00:16:19.680
And they are very clear about,

00:16:19.681 --> 00:16:23.955
sort of, what their practices
were in managing accounts,

00:16:23.956 --> 00:16:29.505
and they did a good job in
keeping passwords encrypted,

00:16:29.506 --> 00:16:31.500
and so we did not see

00:16:31.501 --> 00:16:35.140
a login to our hard account,
hard password account.

00:16:35.141 --> 00:16:36.535
&gt;&gt; So what's the question mark?

00:16:36.536 --> 00:16:38.300
&gt;&gt; The question mark here is

00:16:38.301 --> 00:16:40.630
that due to
methodological issues,

00:16:40.631 --> 00:16:44.545
we only registered an easy
password account on that site.

00:16:44.546 --> 00:16:47.060
We did not register
a hard one that

00:16:47.061 --> 00:16:49.895
we did not- we only
discovered that afterwards.

00:16:49.896 --> 00:16:51.340
And so we don't know.

00:16:51.341 --> 00:16:53.610
That's why we leave
a question mark.

00:16:53.611 --> 00:16:55.230
&gt;&gt; So when you checked,

00:16:55.231 --> 00:16:57.210
that means the
hardware was like,

00:16:57.211 --> 00:16:59.980
in the cases where
the hard password was tried,

00:16:59.981 --> 00:17:01.370
was the easy one also tried?

00:17:01.371 --> 00:17:04.895
&gt;&gt; In general yes,
both were tried.

00:17:04.896 --> 00:17:06.180
Not always, but in general, yes.

00:17:06.181 --> 00:17:12.070
&gt;&gt; So why, well,

00:17:12.071 --> 00:17:14.620
why would one, not
the other fits?

00:17:14.621 --> 00:17:16.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah, they're going through

00:17:16.531 --> 00:17:18.600
all of the accounts sort
of systematically while,

00:17:18.601 --> 00:17:20.070
when they do all of them.

00:17:20.071 --> 00:17:21.945
I don't have
a good answer for that.

00:17:21.946 --> 00:17:23.670
I think there was one situation,

00:17:23.671 --> 00:17:25.200
one of the sites where the

00:17:25.201 --> 00:17:27.215
easy account was
not logged into.

00:17:27.216 --> 00:17:29.700
&gt;&gt; So just a clarification.

00:17:29.701 --> 00:17:34.895
So when there is a compromise
using a hard password,

00:17:34.896 --> 00:17:37.035
you're able to verify
that they weren't just

00:17:37.036 --> 00:17:39.930
doing brute force attacks
of hard passwords.

00:17:39.931 --> 00:17:42.170
It was just one login attempt
with the exact password.

00:17:42.171 --> 00:17:44.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah. These were
not brute forced.

00:17:44.401 --> 00:17:47.615
&gt;&gt; But the ones where there
isn't a hard check mark,

00:17:47.616 --> 00:17:49.615
they tried to brute force?

00:17:49.616 --> 00:17:51.090
Like, you could you could
see the brute force attack.

00:17:51.091 --> 00:17:53.300
&gt;&gt; They were not doing
any- they were not brute

00:17:53.301 --> 00:17:56.810
forcing account logins
at the webmail provider.

00:17:56.811 --> 00:17:57.280
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:17:57.281 --> 00:17:58.930
&gt;&gt; What they were doing
is, they would take

00:17:58.931 --> 00:18:01.160
the password database off

00:18:01.161 --> 00:18:04.580
line and then they would
brute force it off line,

00:18:04.581 --> 00:18:08.340
and then they would just use
what it is they discovered

00:18:08.341 --> 00:18:11.875
as a single login
attempt. Successful.

00:18:11.876 --> 00:18:15.000
&gt;&gt; So for 2,300 websites,

00:18:15.001 --> 00:18:20.850
was there any public
compromise among them,

00:18:20.851 --> 00:18:23.155
that you have account
and you didn't

00:18:23.156 --> 00:18:26.650
see the e-mail account was used?

00:18:26.651 --> 00:18:30.310
&gt;&gt; So there were no,

00:18:30.311 --> 00:18:34.000
there were no sites that
had declared themselves

00:18:34.001 --> 00:18:35.400
as being compromised where

00:18:35.401 --> 00:18:37.455
we had an account
and did not see it.

00:18:37.456 --> 00:18:39.660
So there were no, we
didn't miss anything,

00:18:39.661 --> 00:18:42.715
there were no false negatives.
We didn't miss anything.

00:18:42.716 --> 00:18:45.810
&gt;&gt; We should be talking
more about what about

00:18:45.811 --> 00:18:48.020
these sites, you know, widely.

00:18:48.021 --> 00:18:54.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah. And it's,

00:18:54.921 --> 00:18:56.710
you know it's easy to imagine,

00:18:56.711 --> 00:18:58.730
it's sort of a random
hobby site out there.

00:18:58.731 --> 00:19:01.965
Doesn't have good security
practices and can be breached.

00:19:01.966 --> 00:19:03.790
But for the sites we're in,

00:19:03.791 --> 00:19:06.790
the Alexa top 500 in
their home country.

00:19:06.791 --> 00:19:11.115
So even top sites
like the first one,

00:19:11.116 --> 00:19:13.800
top 500 Alexa ranking,

00:19:13.801 --> 00:19:17.455
billion dollar company, and

00:19:17.456 --> 00:19:20.915
the hard password account
was accessed.

00:19:20.916 --> 00:19:24.490
And then for these two,

00:19:24.491 --> 00:19:27.440
they wound up being
the same company totaling,

00:19:27.441 --> 00:19:30.275
comprising about 30
million users but

00:19:30.276 --> 00:19:35.570
only the easy password
was accessed there. Yes.

00:19:35.571 --> 00:19:37.710
&gt;&gt; So when the easy
password was accessed,

00:19:37.711 --> 00:19:39.725
your hypothesis is that it was

00:19:39.726 --> 00:19:41.775
just an online guessing attack

00:19:41.776 --> 00:19:44.170
at the site in question, right?

00:19:44.171 --> 00:19:46.440
&gt;&gt; Offline guessing, until they

00:19:46.441 --> 00:19:48.620
had figured out passwords,

00:19:48.621 --> 00:19:50.010
and then they would just use

00:19:50.011 --> 00:19:51.645
those passwords at
the webmail provider.

00:19:51.646 --> 00:19:54.010
&gt;&gt; For the weak password?

00:19:54.011 --> 00:19:56.110
&gt;&gt; For the weak password, yes.

00:19:56.111 --> 00:19:59.890
&gt;&gt; So why wouldn't you
assume that it was an online,

00:19:59.891 --> 00:20:02.700
just a guessing attack
against the server,

00:20:02.701 --> 00:20:04.870
and there was
no compromise other than,

00:20:04.871 --> 00:20:06.800
like, how weak were
those passwords?

00:20:06.801 --> 00:20:09.360
&gt;&gt; I see. So the question is,

00:20:09.361 --> 00:20:11.350
for the sites that
were compromised,

00:20:11.351 --> 00:20:13.320
were those sites brute
forced externally?

00:20:13.321 --> 00:20:16.255
&gt;&gt; No. The question
is for the ones,

00:20:16.256 --> 00:20:21.340
for the sites where
the weak account was taken,

00:20:21.341 --> 00:20:24.170
are you saying that it was,

00:20:24.171 --> 00:20:28.600
are you suggesting
that that account was

00:20:28.601 --> 00:20:31.020
compromised because
somebody guessed and

00:20:31.021 --> 00:20:33.715
password was so easy that
it was guessable online?

00:20:33.716 --> 00:20:35.110
Or that there was breach with

00:20:35.111 --> 00:20:36.920
the server and they got
under the knife like-

00:20:36.921 --> 00:20:38.530
&gt;&gt;So that would
be a possibility,

00:20:38.531 --> 00:20:40.080
which is that the account was so

00:20:40.081 --> 00:20:42.550
easy that they would just
go to the webmail provider,

00:20:42.551 --> 00:20:43.895
and just use,

00:20:43.896 --> 00:20:46.160
guess very simply that
they could just login.

00:20:46.161 --> 00:20:47.010
&gt;&gt; Yup.

00:20:47.011 --> 00:20:48.280
&gt;&gt; There are two reasons why

00:20:48.281 --> 00:20:49.320
we don't think
that was the case.

00:20:49.321 --> 00:20:50.520
The first is that the webmail

00:20:50.521 --> 00:20:52.120
provider check to see if there

00:20:52.121 --> 00:20:53.940
is any sort of
brute force attempts

00:20:53.941 --> 00:20:55.250
on any of the accounts,

00:20:55.251 --> 00:20:58.025
multiple logins, before it
actually was successful.

00:20:58.026 --> 00:21:01.260
&gt;&gt; Not but on
online attack at the site?

00:21:01.261 --> 00:21:02.100
&gt;&gt; At the site.

00:21:02.101 --> 00:21:03.340
&gt;&gt; They would have had to guess

00:21:03.341 --> 00:21:06.195
his randomly generated
email address as well as-

00:21:06.196 --> 00:21:07.350
&gt;&gt; That's what I'm getting to.

00:21:07.351 --> 00:21:10.520
&gt;&gt; So all of our e-mail
addresses are private.

00:21:10.521 --> 00:21:13.810
&gt;&gt; Okay, and they're
not super discoverable?

00:21:13.811 --> 00:21:14.080
&gt;&gt; No.

00:21:14.081 --> 00:21:15.470
&gt;&gt; That's good.

00:21:15.471 --> 00:21:17.060
But they are
themselves constraints?

00:21:17.061 --> 00:21:21.370
&gt;&gt; Yes. Yes, exactly. All right.

00:21:21.371 --> 00:21:24.190
So this is, this just
shows sort of from

00:21:24.191 --> 00:21:25.470
a timeline perspective what

00:21:25.471 --> 00:21:28.215
the login activity it is
across the different sites.

00:21:28.216 --> 00:21:31.030
So this is time on the x axis,

00:21:31.031 --> 00:21:34.820
the different sites are
each a row going across.

00:21:34.821 --> 00:21:36.200
And then we have two dots,

00:21:36.201 --> 00:21:40.370
an open circle for
easy, orange for hard.

00:21:40.371 --> 00:21:42.435
And it just shows
you, over time,

00:21:42.436 --> 00:21:44.450
what the login activity across
the different sites were.

00:21:44.451 --> 00:21:45.605
&gt;&gt; How many times?

00:21:45.606 --> 00:21:50.200
&gt;&gt; And then, the number in
parentheses on the right,

00:21:50.201 --> 00:21:52.520
y, is the is the number
of logins per site.

00:21:52.521 --> 00:21:55.525
&gt;&gt; These are email logins?

00:21:55.526 --> 00:21:59.690
&gt;&gt; Yes. So these are attackers

00:21:59.691 --> 00:22:01.845
logging into
our e-mail accounts at

00:22:01.846 --> 00:22:03.820
the webmail provider using

00:22:03.821 --> 00:22:04.900
one of the two
different ones to-

00:22:04.901 --> 00:22:06.765
&gt;&gt; And when did you ask?

00:22:06.766 --> 00:22:09.030
So 7/15 is your first.

00:22:09.031 --> 00:22:09.855
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:22:09.856 --> 00:22:12.615
&gt;&gt; And when did you first
set this whole thing up?

00:22:12.616 --> 00:22:17.140
&gt;&gt; 7/15, I think was
three months after we started.

00:22:17.141 --> 00:22:18.530
&gt;&gt; So what did you

00:22:18.531 --> 00:22:20.785
think happened for
three months, nothing?

00:22:20.786 --> 00:22:23.590
&gt;&gt; Well, we did have a gap

00:22:23.591 --> 00:22:27.865
in that period where it could
have been that there are,

00:22:27.866 --> 00:22:30.640
accounts had been compromised
and we didn't detect it.

00:22:30.641 --> 00:22:34.930
I have no good
explanation for- but it's,

00:22:34.931 --> 00:22:41.105
you know, we sorted them by
time of first account access.

00:22:41.106 --> 00:22:43.440
And you do see that
there are gaps in

00:22:43.441 --> 00:22:47.275
months before we detect
another compromise.

00:22:47.276 --> 00:22:50.305
So it's going to be in
a month's time frame.

00:22:50.306 --> 00:22:51.360
&gt;&gt; Kind of weird how-

00:22:51.361 --> 00:22:51.980
&gt;&gt; Can I tell?

00:22:51.981 --> 00:22:56.905
&gt;&gt; It looks like a month
when the compromise started.

00:22:56.906 --> 00:23:01.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I don't want to read
too much in the tea leaves.

00:23:01.861 --> 00:23:02.980
&gt;&gt; What are they doing

00:23:02.981 --> 00:23:04.590
when they login, are
they sending spam?

00:23:04.591 --> 00:23:08.080
&gt;&gt; I thought this

00:23:08.081 --> 00:23:09.940
was going to be an
interesting part of the study,

00:23:09.941 --> 00:23:12.170
which is looking to see
what the attackers did with

00:23:12.171 --> 00:23:13.470
the accounts and
then we end up being

00:23:13.471 --> 00:23:15.035
an uninteresting part
of the study.

00:23:15.036 --> 00:23:17.960
There were a few where
the automated systems that

00:23:17.961 --> 00:23:19.580
the webmail providers to shut

00:23:19.581 --> 00:23:21.715
down the accounts because
they were sending out spam,

00:23:21.716 --> 00:23:23.310
but by and large,

00:23:23.311 --> 00:23:26.115
they would login, look
around, and just not do much.

00:23:26.116 --> 00:23:28.720
And they're doing
this multiple times.

00:23:28.721 --> 00:23:32.130
So, I just don't know. I think.

00:23:32.131 --> 00:23:34.320
&gt;&gt; It's not that much
in the email inbox.

00:23:34.321 --> 00:23:36.035
&gt;&gt; We did not. But
it's clear that

00:23:36.036 --> 00:23:37.900
they were logging in
repeatedly perhaps

00:23:37.901 --> 00:23:39.890
to test to see whether or
not the password had been

00:23:39.891 --> 00:23:42.810
changed and whether
the inventory was still live.

00:23:42.811 --> 00:23:45.790
&gt;&gt; Was the webmail provider
a top tier one?

00:23:45.791 --> 00:23:47.515
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:23:47.516 --> 00:23:50.580
&gt;&gt; Look, could it be

00:23:50.581 --> 00:23:54.140
that they were just checking
the liveness of the account,

00:23:54.141 --> 00:23:55.490
so that they can sell it?

00:23:55.491 --> 00:23:56.080
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:23:56.081 --> 00:23:58.815
&gt;&gt; Verified account
with a working password.

00:23:58.816 --> 00:24:01.210
&gt;&gt; That's the best hypothesis

00:24:01.211 --> 00:24:03.380
I can think of for
the activity that we saw.

00:24:03.381 --> 00:24:05.405
I thought we were going
to be able to watch

00:24:05.406 --> 00:24:08.440
all this crazy stuff
going on with the account,

00:24:08.441 --> 00:24:11.095
it wound up being
totally uninteresting.

00:24:11.096 --> 00:24:16.110
&gt;&gt; What's special about M?

00:24:16.111 --> 00:24:19.190
&gt;&gt; Yes. So there

00:24:19.191 --> 00:24:21.630
are a couple where there's
just crazy stuff going

00:24:21.631 --> 00:24:22.720
on in some of the accounts

00:24:22.721 --> 00:24:27.265
and the time between
logins is very, very short.

00:24:27.266 --> 00:24:31.745
I don't know.
One hypothesis is that

00:24:31.746 --> 00:24:33.800
those credentials
were given to lots of

00:24:33.801 --> 00:24:35.300
different people
and so you just saw

00:24:35.301 --> 00:24:37.715
lots of different people
trying over time.

00:24:37.716 --> 00:24:42.480
&gt;&gt; So the numbers
on the right are

00:24:42.481 --> 00:24:46.600
they both unique email accounts
or unique websites?

00:24:46.601 --> 00:24:49.725
&gt;&gt; The numbers on
the right are the number of

00:24:49.726 --> 00:24:52.425
unique successful logins

00:24:52.426 --> 00:24:55.260
to the email account
associated with these sites.

00:24:55.261 --> 00:24:57.600
&gt;&gt; Under the same,

00:24:57.601 --> 00:25:00.580
because I thought you registered

00:25:00.581 --> 00:25:03.640
a lot of email accounts so
is it under the same account?

00:25:03.641 --> 00:25:04.890
&gt;&gt; The same account.

00:25:04.891 --> 00:25:08.440
So just to be very
concrete, for A,

00:25:08.441 --> 00:25:11.170
we saw we had two accounts
and we saw two logins,

00:25:11.171 --> 00:25:13.175
one to the easy,
one to the hard,

00:25:13.176 --> 00:25:15.845
and then we never
saw logins again.

00:25:15.846 --> 00:25:20.320
And then for N which had 570,

00:25:20.321 --> 00:25:22.020
again we just had two accounts,

00:25:22.021 --> 00:25:24.880
but we saw 570 logins
to those accounts.

00:25:24.881 --> 00:25:27.125
&gt;&gt; Why did you not register

00:25:27.126 --> 00:25:30.905
more hard and more
easy accounts?

00:25:30.906 --> 00:25:33.115
Why did you just limit to one?

00:25:33.116 --> 00:25:35.980
&gt;&gt; So it's a good question.

00:25:35.981 --> 00:25:37.990
The value of registering
more would be

00:25:37.991 --> 00:25:39.880
greater confidence
that the attackers

00:25:39.881 --> 00:25:42.200
were just going through
an entire password database.

00:25:42.201 --> 00:25:44.660
But the flip side is that
we wanted to minimize

00:25:44.661 --> 00:25:47.175
harm so to speak to
the sites under question,

00:25:47.176 --> 00:25:48.910
because we didn't
ask them if we could

00:25:48.911 --> 00:25:49.945
register honey accounts with

00:25:49.946 --> 00:25:51.345
them we were just doing that.

00:25:51.346 --> 00:25:53.760
And so minimizing resources and

00:25:53.761 --> 00:25:56.815
impact on them for
the work that we're doing,

00:25:56.816 --> 00:25:59.470
lead us to minimize the number
of accounts that we have.

00:25:59.471 --> 00:26:02.090
&gt;&gt; The reason I'm asking

00:26:02.091 --> 00:26:05.160
this it's interesting
that one of them had

00:26:05.161 --> 00:26:11.325
a lot of hard logins but
no single easy log in. Right.

00:26:11.326 --> 00:26:12.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:26:12.041 --> 00:26:18.945
&gt;&gt; So one question I think,

00:26:18.946 --> 00:26:21.480
you saw, look at M,

00:26:21.481 --> 00:26:24.435
you saw the first
compromise around 6/16,

00:26:24.436 --> 00:26:27.520
and you didn't warn

00:26:27.521 --> 00:26:31.795
their site and they kept
compromising until 270.

00:26:31.796 --> 00:26:33.410
&gt;&gt; Okay. So what I have not

00:26:33.411 --> 00:26:35.630
plotted is when it
was that we notified.

00:26:35.631 --> 00:26:36.955
For some of the sites,

00:26:36.956 --> 00:26:38.905
it took us a little
while to notify.

00:26:38.906 --> 00:26:41.530
And the reason why was

00:26:41.531 --> 00:26:46.340
that the webmail provider
was doing this for us as

00:26:46.341 --> 00:26:50.550
a favor and it required

00:26:50.551 --> 00:26:52.980
somebody's time and
intervention to go in and

00:26:52.981 --> 00:26:55.485
actually collect log data
and send it to us.

00:26:55.486 --> 00:26:57.160
And that only
happened on occasion.

00:26:57.161 --> 00:27:00.920
So there was a delay
in some times between

00:27:00.921 --> 00:27:04.700
when we got it and when we
notified, things like that.

00:27:04.701 --> 00:27:06.630
But it is also the case

00:27:06.631 --> 00:27:11.825
that for some of the sites
we re-registered,

00:27:11.826 --> 00:27:14.160
you know, for all these sites
we re-registered

00:27:14.161 --> 00:27:15.280
new accounts to see

00:27:15.281 --> 00:27:19.160
if there was
subsequent compromises.

00:27:19.161 --> 00:27:21.220
And in some of the sites cases,
that didn't happen.

00:27:21.221 --> 00:27:23.250
&gt;&gt; So did you do

00:27:23.251 --> 00:27:24.600
any study of whether or not they

00:27:24.601 --> 00:27:26.290
were mining the inboxes also?

00:27:26.291 --> 00:27:31.330
&gt;&gt; We could not get information,

00:27:31.331 --> 00:27:32.890
detailed information
about what they were

00:27:32.891 --> 00:27:34.890
doing with the inbox

00:27:34.891 --> 00:27:37.950
and we had not salted

00:27:37.951 --> 00:27:39.865
the inbox with
that much information.

00:27:39.866 --> 00:27:41.720
Because the challenge is we've

00:27:41.721 --> 00:27:44.610
created 200,000 accounts
something like that,

00:27:44.611 --> 00:27:48.090
and doing that at scale winds
up being very difficult.

00:27:48.091 --> 00:27:50.600
We did create
realistic profiles for

00:27:50.601 --> 00:27:53.690
all the individuals
with names, addresses,

00:27:53.691 --> 00:27:57.165
all of that stuff was all valid,

00:27:57.166 --> 00:28:00.550
but the inbox itself
was uninteresting.

00:28:00.551 --> 00:28:02.090
&gt;&gt; What about outbox?

00:28:02.091 --> 00:28:03.075
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry.

00:28:03.076 --> 00:28:08.950
&gt;&gt; Outbox. Did they use you
to send email to somebody?

00:28:08.951 --> 00:28:10.075
&gt;&gt; Some of them did.

00:28:10.076 --> 00:28:11.875
And there were a couple where

00:28:11.876 --> 00:28:14.075
they were sending
spam at such a rate

00:28:14.076 --> 00:28:16.690
that automated systems
for the webmail provider

00:28:16.691 --> 00:28:19.630
just shut down the account
even without our intervention.

00:28:19.631 --> 00:28:21.080
But by and large,

00:28:21.081 --> 00:28:23.715
there was very very little
activity out of your accounts.

00:28:23.716 --> 00:28:29.420
I was expecting much more
but they were not being used.

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:39.090
So then, once we

00:28:39.091 --> 00:28:40.560
had gathered this information

00:28:40.561 --> 00:28:42.725
and then sort of
rolling over time,

00:28:42.726 --> 00:28:46.115
when we discovered
a site was compromised,

00:28:46.116 --> 00:28:49.100
we would notify the site
and we would look to try

00:28:49.101 --> 00:28:51.930
to find the appropriate
contact addresses,

00:28:51.931 --> 00:28:58.110
we would send them email
and wait for a response.

00:28:58.111 --> 00:29:00.740
And whenever we got

00:29:00.741 --> 00:29:01.770
a response and for

00:29:01.771 --> 00:29:04.785
large sites we got
an immediate response,

00:29:04.786 --> 00:29:08.120
not just from engineering
but also from legal.

00:29:08.121 --> 00:29:12.470
And we were immediately

00:29:12.471 --> 00:29:14.760
as helpful as possible
explaining what we did,

00:29:14.761 --> 00:29:17.400
what we found,
all this kind of stuff.

00:29:17.401 --> 00:29:20.715
But it led to some
interesting conversations

00:29:20.716 --> 00:29:24.305
which provide some insight
into some of these sites.

00:29:24.306 --> 00:29:26.215
So I'll just go
through some examples.

00:29:26.216 --> 00:29:30.055
So I do want to
preface this that

00:29:30.056 --> 00:29:32.290
there was a part of me that
was actually feeling pretty

00:29:32.291 --> 00:29:34.745
bad when interacting
with these folks,

00:29:34.746 --> 00:29:36.240
because we were telling them

00:29:36.241 --> 00:29:37.810
that they had been compromised,

00:29:37.811 --> 00:29:40.285
but we had no insight how or
why they were compromised.

00:29:40.286 --> 00:29:41.925
All we could do was
to detect. It's like,

00:29:41.926 --> 00:29:44.640
"You've been owned, good luck."

00:29:44.641 --> 00:29:47.815
So it puts them in
a slightly awkward position.

00:29:47.816 --> 00:29:49.590
Only a third of the sites

00:29:49.591 --> 00:29:51.015
actually wound up
responding to us.

00:29:51.016 --> 00:29:52.600
But all of the big sites well,

00:29:52.601 --> 00:29:55.095
the majority of
the big sites did.

00:29:55.096 --> 00:29:58.160
In the end though, even

00:29:58.161 --> 00:30:00.535
based upon what we
were able to tell them,

00:30:00.536 --> 00:30:04.520
no sites ever notified
users and no sites forced

00:30:04.521 --> 00:30:08.660
a password reset on
the accounts at their site.

00:30:08.661 --> 00:30:10.550
So it was not enough
to get them to do

00:30:10.551 --> 00:30:11.490
anything and I'll
talk a little bit

00:30:11.491 --> 00:30:12.520
more about that in the summary.

00:30:12.521 --> 00:30:14.810
&gt;&gt; So when you are
notifying them,

00:30:14.811 --> 00:30:16.800
did any of the sites
inform you that it was

00:30:16.801 --> 00:30:18.040
against the policy for

00:30:18.041 --> 00:30:19.585
you to even set up
such crap accounts.

00:30:19.586 --> 00:30:22.675
&gt;&gt; No.

00:30:22.676 --> 00:30:25.685
&gt;&gt;They should done
your job at all?

00:30:25.686 --> 00:30:28.180
&gt;&gt; It could be that

00:30:28.181 --> 00:30:33.315
the acceptable use policy
at the site said,

00:30:33.316 --> 00:30:34.610
"Oh, you cannot use

00:30:34.611 --> 00:30:37.600
an automated system to
register an account."

00:30:37.601 --> 00:30:39.590
And then, when we notified them,

00:30:39.591 --> 00:30:41.620
their response might be,
"Hey, what are you doing?

00:30:41.621 --> 00:30:43.810
We told you, in our
acceptable use policy it says,

00:30:43.811 --> 00:30:44.860
you are not supposed
to be doing that,

00:30:44.861 --> 00:30:45.405
what are you doing?"

00:30:45.406 --> 00:30:47.390
Nobody went down that path.

00:30:47.391 --> 00:30:50.530
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] if I'm

00:30:50.531 --> 00:30:53.680
a big company I really have
to take your word and say,

00:30:53.681 --> 00:30:55.710
"Oh this counter was never used,

00:30:55.711 --> 00:30:57.110
they are only storing here".

00:30:57.111 --> 00:30:59.915
Right. I could
very well just say,

00:30:59.916 --> 00:31:03.410
"You used it somewhere, and
it was leaked, not from us.

00:31:03.411 --> 00:31:05.130
So why should I tell

00:31:05.131 --> 00:31:07.905
the 100 million users
just because one of them?"

00:31:07.906 --> 00:31:12.340
&gt;&gt; So we'll talk about
the top company in a second.

00:31:12.341 --> 00:31:15.030
Okay. But, let's start small.

00:31:15.031 --> 00:31:17.765
So going into this,

00:31:17.766 --> 00:31:19.815
we fully expected that we

00:31:19.816 --> 00:31:23.360
would find sites that were
well down the Alexa rankings,

00:31:23.361 --> 00:31:28.280
that it's run by an individual
or small group of people.

00:31:28.281 --> 00:31:30.580
It's not as if they have
a giant security team and

00:31:30.581 --> 00:31:32.150
sure they would
likely have lots of

00:31:32.151 --> 00:31:33.980
problems and sure enough yes,

00:31:33.981 --> 00:31:35.430
we did run into those problems

00:31:35.431 --> 00:31:37.345
and sites that had
those problems.

00:31:37.346 --> 00:31:40.170
In interacting with them.
They would promise to fix

00:31:40.171 --> 00:31:42.540
it soon but never took action.

00:31:42.541 --> 00:31:45.755
The bottom line is they're
too busy fighting other fires.

00:31:45.756 --> 00:31:48.470
So this was a great
anecdote from one of

00:31:48.471 --> 00:31:51.820
the sites which was like,

00:31:51.821 --> 00:31:53.240
"Look, when we started

00:31:53.241 --> 00:31:55.040
this site I used
to have sysadmins,

00:31:55.041 --> 00:31:56.810
but they kept on telling me that

00:31:56.811 --> 00:31:59.160
I couldn't do things,
so I fired them all.

00:31:59.161 --> 00:32:04.320
And that caused
even more problems for me."

00:32:04.321 --> 00:32:10.095
Well, probably not surprising
that this occurred.

00:32:10.096 --> 00:32:13.320
For the large sites,

00:32:13.321 --> 00:32:16.905
they were very responsive
except for one.

00:32:16.906 --> 00:32:21.890
And we heard from

00:32:21.891 --> 00:32:23.200
different parts of the company

00:32:23.201 --> 00:32:24.910
both engineering and legal.

00:32:24.911 --> 00:32:29.160
And for the very top site,
the very big one,

00:32:29.161 --> 00:32:33.500
we had multiple phone
calls with them,

00:32:33.501 --> 00:32:36.780
where their lawyer just

00:32:36.781 --> 00:32:38.960
asks us a long series
of questions.

00:32:38.961 --> 00:32:40.410
And the idea behind

00:32:40.411 --> 00:32:41.760
the questions, the goal
behind the questions,

00:32:41.761 --> 00:32:43.250
was to try to find a weakness in

00:32:43.251 --> 00:32:45.050
our methodology such
that they could point to

00:32:45.051 --> 00:32:46.930
that as being
the explanation for

00:32:46.931 --> 00:32:51.175
the account compromise not
to the site being breached.

00:32:51.176 --> 00:32:57.180
Joe is fantastic
and handled that

00:32:57.181 --> 00:32:59.360
questioning like a pro
and they could not

00:32:59.361 --> 00:33:03.065
find a chink in the armor.

00:33:03.066 --> 00:33:05.750
They still did not
corroborate that

00:33:05.751 --> 00:33:08.450
there was a compromise.

00:33:08.451 --> 00:33:12.240
The last question we
would ask is, well then,

00:33:12.241 --> 00:33:14.590
what is another explanation
for all these that we found,

00:33:14.591 --> 00:33:16.720
given everything
that we've discussed,

00:33:16.721 --> 00:33:18.825
and they would never
answer the question.

00:33:18.826 --> 00:33:24.455
So, for this Outdoor Site,

00:33:24.456 --> 00:33:27.440
it was a Site that went
up being purchased by.

00:33:27.441 --> 00:33:29.485
&gt;&gt; So there are some kind of

00:33:29.486 --> 00:33:30.665
small companies that are go

00:33:30.666 --> 00:33:32.225
ravaging around in the dark web,

00:33:32.226 --> 00:33:33.760
find stuff on Pastebin,

00:33:33.761 --> 00:33:35.400
and then contact companies

00:33:35.401 --> 00:33:36.690
saying I've good evidence
that you've been

00:33:36.691 --> 00:33:37.980
breached as a way of kind of

00:33:37.981 --> 00:33:40.545
like trying to sell
their consulting stuff.

00:33:40.546 --> 00:33:41.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah. FYI.

00:33:41.831 --> 00:33:43.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Did anyone kind of

00:33:43.571 --> 00:33:45.480
indicate or hint
that they might have

00:33:45.481 --> 00:33:47.990
had because there's a chance

00:33:47.991 --> 00:33:48.500
that there might have been

00:33:48.501 --> 00:33:49.900
other channels that
this would feed backed?

00:33:49.901 --> 00:33:51.870
&gt;&gt; Yes, one of the sites
knew they had been breached,

00:33:51.871 --> 00:33:54.920
because somebody posted to
their forum saying, "Hey,

00:33:54.921 --> 00:33:57.960
we breached and we
duplicated your entire site

00:33:57.961 --> 00:33:59.460
and the alternate site
is running over

00:33:59.461 --> 00:34:01.615
here in case you want to
come over and use us."

00:34:01.616 --> 00:34:03.110
&gt;&gt; And there's
a separate kind of a,

00:34:03.111 --> 00:34:04.340
maybe your stats is getting

00:34:04.341 --> 00:34:05.600
kind of a little
because, you know,

00:34:05.601 --> 00:34:06.870
at 19, but it would
be interesting to

00:34:06.871 --> 00:34:09.705
see all those that are bridges
assuming all of these are.

00:34:09.706 --> 00:34:11.800
What percent actually
end up on kind of

00:34:11.801 --> 00:34:14.290
scriptable places like
Pastebin as opposed to,

00:34:14.291 --> 00:34:16.410
I'm going to use
this for myself and

00:34:16.411 --> 00:34:17.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:17.331 --> 00:34:20.000
&gt;&gt; But the question
the efficacy of

00:34:20.001 --> 00:34:21.430
those services because like for

00:34:21.431 --> 00:34:23.540
example, like
Microsoft employees,

00:34:23.541 --> 00:34:26.265
if you sign up for
the Legal Plan,

00:34:26.266 --> 00:34:28.390
one of the things
that they offer is,

00:34:28.391 --> 00:34:32.720
to tell you if your e-mail
account has been compromised.

00:34:32.721 --> 00:34:34.240
And the way that
they do that is,

00:34:34.241 --> 00:34:35.870
you enter your personal
e-mail address,

00:34:35.871 --> 00:34:37.735
in my case it's
strategymobile.com

00:34:37.736 --> 00:34:38.940
and then I regularly

00:34:38.941 --> 00:34:40.305
get like reports from
them saying that,

00:34:40.306 --> 00:34:42.070
we find a website where there

00:34:42.071 --> 00:34:43.985
was your email address
and some password.

00:34:43.986 --> 00:34:45.310
And I have no idea whether

00:34:45.311 --> 00:34:48.480
that password is cracked or not.

00:34:48.481 --> 00:34:50.470
&gt;&gt; And I would say the

00:34:50.471 --> 00:34:53.540
lesson that we learned
from the small sites is,

00:34:53.541 --> 00:34:55.660
even if they are notified
whether it's from

00:34:55.661 --> 00:34:57.870
us or whether these
other channels,

00:34:57.871 --> 00:34:59.700
it's a high enough
on our priority

00:34:59.701 --> 00:35:01.060
to do something about it.

00:35:01.061 --> 00:35:03.050
Their dealing with
other fires. Yeah.

00:35:03.051 --> 00:35:04.825
&gt;&gt; So there's a database that

00:35:04.826 --> 00:35:06.970
is called have I been
[inaudible] with a p.com.

00:35:06.971 --> 00:35:07.560
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:35:07.561 --> 00:35:08.895
&gt;&gt; Or net or something and

00:35:08.896 --> 00:35:10.850
that actually works
quite well because

00:35:10.851 --> 00:35:13.180
I generally use
unique e-mail address

00:35:13.181 --> 00:35:14.980
for all the sites
that I register with.

00:35:14.981 --> 00:35:17.870
And they tell me like
when I elected one

00:35:17.871 --> 00:35:19.410
of the Adobe one
and all the ones

00:35:19.411 --> 00:35:20.650
that are known to
be compromised,

00:35:20.651 --> 00:35:22.230
I've received a
notification through them.

00:35:22.231 --> 00:35:25.130
So my question is,
have you considered

00:35:25.131 --> 00:35:27.450
cross-referencing
your known hacked

00:35:27.451 --> 00:35:28.680
accounts with that database?

00:35:28.681 --> 00:35:29.870
&gt;&gt; Now that's a good question

00:35:29.871 --> 00:35:30.760
and we didn't do it by have-

00:35:30.761 --> 00:35:31.870
&gt;&gt; We can sign up.

00:35:31.871 --> 00:35:34.985
&gt;&gt; Yeah, very good thing to
do. Yeah. It's good point.

00:35:34.986 --> 00:35:37.495
Okay, so for this site,

00:35:37.496 --> 00:35:40.030
the site was purchased

00:35:40.031 --> 00:35:42.495
by a competitor in
a competing site.

00:35:42.496 --> 00:35:44.880
And at the time

00:35:44.881 --> 00:35:46.900
the site was handed
over to the competitor,

00:35:46.901 --> 00:35:49.350
that is when we saw
the account being

00:35:49.351 --> 00:35:52.325
accessed from the original site.

00:35:52.326 --> 00:35:54.180
And so, who know you know

00:35:54.181 --> 00:35:55.590
that might have been
an insider thing,

00:35:55.591 --> 00:35:57.755
not necessarily an
external site breach,

00:35:57.756 --> 00:35:59.515
who knows what was
going on there.

00:35:59.516 --> 00:36:02.595
And then, for the very top one,

00:36:02.596 --> 00:36:06.970
there's billion dollar company,
50 million users.

00:36:06.971 --> 00:36:09.240
So we did have

00:36:09.241 --> 00:36:12.825
extended conversations
with them over time.

00:36:12.826 --> 00:36:15.880
After we had gone
through this, sort of,

00:36:15.881 --> 00:36:20.315
entire methodology and
they couldn't find a flaw.

00:36:20.316 --> 00:36:22.270
The next call that
we had with them

00:36:22.271 --> 00:36:24.370
is they said that they had hired

00:36:24.371 --> 00:36:27.200
a third party instant

00:36:27.201 --> 00:36:28.780
rooflines team to review

00:36:28.781 --> 00:36:30.705
everything and review
their internals.

00:36:30.706 --> 00:36:32.455
And the third party team found

00:36:32.456 --> 00:36:35.970
no indication of
site compromise.

00:36:35.971 --> 00:36:38.980
But this was a site
that at the time that

00:36:38.981 --> 00:36:41.950
our logins we observed
log in's to our accounts,

00:36:41.951 --> 00:36:44.160
other users were
complaining on Twitter,

00:36:44.161 --> 00:36:47.720
that their logins on
the site were compromised,

00:36:47.721 --> 00:36:51.940
and there was even a story
in a newspaper about

00:36:51.941 --> 00:36:53.600
it but the site

00:36:53.601 --> 00:36:57.380
denied the allegations that
they would compromised.

00:36:57.381 --> 00:37:00.310
So for this one actually we had

00:37:00.311 --> 00:37:07.020
some additional external
validation that actually,

00:37:07.021 --> 00:37:08.380
it wasn't just our accounts

00:37:08.381 --> 00:37:10.580
who they were
compromising this time.

00:37:10.581 --> 00:37:15.430
Okay. An interesting
side effect of these,

00:37:15.431 --> 00:37:17.735
was that particularly
for this site,

00:37:17.736 --> 00:37:20.040
I was sort of waiting for
something like this sort of

00:37:20.041 --> 00:37:21.230
a legal document handed to

00:37:21.231 --> 00:37:23.560
us and we did get
a Cease and Desist order.

00:37:23.561 --> 00:37:26.380
But it wasn't from any of
the sites that we monitor.

00:37:26.381 --> 00:37:27.890
It was from Tripwire Inc.,

00:37:27.891 --> 00:37:29.360
who were very angry with

00:37:29.361 --> 00:37:31.490
us for calling
our system Tripwire.

00:37:31.491 --> 00:37:38.395
And it's like, it's just,

00:37:38.396 --> 00:37:41.125
it's a name. We're not
commercializing it.

00:37:41.126 --> 00:37:45.890
Okay. So, the technique works.

00:37:45.891 --> 00:37:48.540
We did a pilot study found in

00:37:48.541 --> 00:37:50.630
our roughly 19 compromised site,

00:37:50.631 --> 00:37:53.180
one percent of the sites
that we were looking at it.

00:37:53.181 --> 00:37:54.670
Compromisers were happening at

00:37:54.671 --> 00:37:56.300
sites of all different scales.

00:37:56.301 --> 00:37:58.280
The small ones not so surprising
but actually there were

00:37:58.281 --> 00:37:59.820
large ones that didn't

00:37:59.821 --> 00:38:02.280
have sort of the
greatest practices.

00:38:02.281 --> 00:38:03.990
And none of this, none of

00:38:03.991 --> 00:38:05.440
the sites did anything with

00:38:05.441 --> 00:38:07.315
their users as a result of this.

00:38:07.316 --> 00:38:13.750
And there's strong incentives

00:38:13.751 --> 00:38:16.975
not to state that your site
has been compromised.

00:38:16.976 --> 00:38:19.245
And so you can imagine
from their perspective,

00:38:19.246 --> 00:38:22.200
if they had been
compromised, the risk,

00:38:22.201 --> 00:38:25.190
the impact on stock prices
all of those kind of stuff of

00:38:25.191 --> 00:38:26.870
declaring that versus sort

00:38:26.871 --> 00:38:29.465
of a research team coming
in and saying look,

00:38:29.466 --> 00:38:30.940
we have no other
explanation other

00:38:30.941 --> 00:38:32.240
than your site has
been compromised with

00:38:32.241 --> 00:38:33.740
no additional
information that they're

00:38:33.741 --> 00:38:37.072
willing to take the risk to
say that it didn't happen.

00:38:37.073 --> 00:38:43.885
&gt;&gt; So, earlier when you
talked about the difference

00:38:43.886 --> 00:38:47.005
between hard and easy passwords,

00:38:47.006 --> 00:38:50.410
the fact that somebody

00:38:50.411 --> 00:38:53.075
would log in to one of
your hard password sites.

00:38:53.076 --> 00:38:56.485
You said that that
indicated that the server

00:38:56.486 --> 00:38:59.915
was potentially having
bad password practices,

00:38:59.916 --> 00:39:02.570
but is that
necessarily the case?

00:39:02.571 --> 00:39:04.100
In other words, once a site is

00:39:04.101 --> 00:39:07.220
compromised depending on
the nature of the compromise,

00:39:07.221 --> 00:39:11.035
I may be able to just
observe people logging in.

00:39:11.036 --> 00:39:14.320
Admittedly, your
particular e-mail account

00:39:14.321 --> 00:39:18.875
nobody would have logged
in but are you sure of it?

00:39:18.876 --> 00:39:20.630
&gt;&gt; It can't be
absolutely certain.

00:39:20.631 --> 00:39:22.000
So let's say it's the case

00:39:22.001 --> 00:39:23.340
that somebody installs Malware,

00:39:23.341 --> 00:39:25.440
at the other end

00:39:25.441 --> 00:39:27.540
the HPS Connections are
actually able to see

00:39:27.541 --> 00:39:30.270
that the authentication
happen in

00:39:30.271 --> 00:39:34.135
real time as in our PC and
whatever, that's potentially.

00:39:34.136 --> 00:39:35.990
We will not log in
to our accounts,

00:39:35.991 --> 00:39:37.490
so they probably
wouldn't see ours.

00:39:37.491 --> 00:39:38.730
So it's hard to have that be

00:39:38.731 --> 00:39:41.040
an explanation for our accounts.

00:39:41.041 --> 00:39:43.240
But that is very much

00:39:43.241 --> 00:39:45.355
a limitation with
this technique which is,

00:39:45.356 --> 00:39:48.225
it gives us no insight
into the compromise itself.

00:39:48.226 --> 00:39:50.580
&gt;&gt; So the following question

00:39:50.581 --> 00:39:53.830
is for sites that

00:39:53.831 --> 00:39:56.270
are interested in detecting
their own compromise,

00:39:56.271 --> 00:39:57.915
would you recommend
this technique to them?

00:39:57.916 --> 00:40:03.160
&gt;&gt; Yes. Absolutely. Yeah.

00:40:05.410 --> 00:40:09.800
OK. All right.

00:40:09.801 --> 00:40:13.470
I clearly created way
too many sites but that's okay.

00:40:13.471 --> 00:40:16.180
I'm going to transition
a little bit,

00:40:16.181 --> 00:40:18.130
I've been talking about sort of

00:40:18.131 --> 00:40:19.920
work that we've been
doing looking at DNS,

00:40:19.921 --> 00:40:21.200
in particular the introduction

00:40:21.201 --> 00:40:22.330
of the new top level domains.

00:40:22.331 --> 00:40:24.210
And this is the work
that our student

00:40:24.211 --> 00:40:26.915
Tristan did as part
of his dissertation.

00:40:26.916 --> 00:40:29.360
There are sort of
two different stages

00:40:29.361 --> 00:40:30.950
of this work and I will talk

00:40:30.951 --> 00:40:32.430
a little bit about the first one

00:40:32.431 --> 00:40:33.540
because I know already that

00:40:33.541 --> 00:40:34.770
I am not going to be able
to do the second one.

00:40:34.771 --> 00:40:37.110
All right, so, here's the idea,

00:40:37.111 --> 00:40:39.715
where we were coming from
in starting this work.

00:40:39.716 --> 00:40:41.730
DNS is absolutely critical

00:40:41.731 --> 00:40:44.100
infrastructure for
the entire internet.

00:40:44.101 --> 00:40:46.845
It is how we name
things on the internet.

00:40:46.846 --> 00:40:50.740
But DNS domain names are
a particular way of being also

00:40:50.741 --> 00:40:52.140
being very essential for

00:40:52.141 --> 00:40:55.200
many different kinds of
large scale abuse and attacks.

00:40:55.201 --> 00:40:56.750
There is value in having

00:40:56.751 --> 00:40:59.545
domain names particularly
if they have reputation.

00:40:59.546 --> 00:41:03.830
So, in a lot of
the work that we did,

00:41:03.831 --> 00:41:06.720
we would look at
domains and the use of

00:41:06.721 --> 00:41:09.660
domains sort of in the context
of a particular attack

00:41:09.661 --> 00:41:12.530
but the introduction
in the new TLDs just

00:41:12.531 --> 00:41:16.170
completely changes
the DNS landscape.

00:41:16.171 --> 00:41:20.860
And so we wanted to
sort of get a sense for

00:41:20.861 --> 00:41:23.000
how this change would

00:41:23.001 --> 00:41:26.225
eventually impact
abuse and attacks.

00:41:26.226 --> 00:41:28.080
And so we started
by just looking at

00:41:28.081 --> 00:41:30.200
what was going on
with the new TLDs.

00:41:30.201 --> 00:41:33.180
All right so that's
for some context.

00:41:33.181 --> 00:41:36.730
Back when we were
in graduate school,

00:41:36.731 --> 00:41:39.060
you had seven TLDs and
the country code TLDs.

00:41:39.061 --> 00:41:42.590
Now, 1,200 new generic TLDs.

00:41:42.591 --> 00:41:46.390
That is a lot. A lot.

00:41:46.391 --> 00:41:48.280
OK. What's going on
with those new TLDs?

00:41:48.281 --> 00:41:50.490
What are they being used for?

00:41:50.491 --> 00:41:59.035
So, you know we wanted to
see to what extent, what,

00:41:59.036 --> 00:42:03.620
what impact these new TLDs
are having on DNS and

00:42:03.621 --> 00:42:06.190
are the introduction
of the new TLDs sort of

00:42:06.191 --> 00:42:07.670
serving the pre-stated purpose

00:42:07.671 --> 00:42:08.650
that they are introduced for.

00:42:08.651 --> 00:42:10.345
Are they meeting unmet needs

00:42:10.346 --> 00:42:12.920
that the lack of those TLDs had.

00:42:12.921 --> 00:42:15.310
And our approach
to sort of doing

00:42:15.311 --> 00:42:17.920
this foundation was to
examine one new TLD in detail

00:42:17.921 --> 00:42:19.890
and then all new

00:42:19.891 --> 00:42:23.060
TLDs that were registered
by the end of 2014.

00:42:23.061 --> 00:42:25.815
So at the time of
this study it was 500 TLDs.

00:42:25.816 --> 00:42:28.215
And we just look at all domains,

00:42:28.216 --> 00:42:30.785
crawl their content,
so on and so forth.

00:42:30.786 --> 00:42:33.160
And then our ongoing work
is sort of looking

00:42:33.161 --> 00:42:36.030
at analyzing DNS from

00:42:36.031 --> 00:42:38.660
a global perspective
to look for abuse and

00:42:38.661 --> 00:42:42.075
attacks and weird ways in
which DNS is being used.

00:42:42.076 --> 00:42:45.710
So I will focus

00:42:45.711 --> 00:42:47.400
on this first one because it

00:42:47.401 --> 00:42:49.960
is again a very
interesting story,

00:42:49.961 --> 00:42:53.045
which is the XXX TLD.

00:42:53.046 --> 00:42:55.620
It's an unusual TLD in

00:42:55.621 --> 00:42:58.300
many ways and it has
a very storied history.

00:42:58.301 --> 00:43:01.170
It's a TLD specialized
for adult content.

00:43:01.171 --> 00:43:04.695
The registry that proposed
it was ICM Registry.

00:43:04.696 --> 00:43:07.040
And they sort of
had a story for what

00:43:07.041 --> 00:43:09.345
the value of the domain was
and all this kind of stuff.

00:43:09.346 --> 00:43:11.670
But there was lots of criticism
all over the place and

00:43:11.671 --> 00:43:14.680
actually even the adult
entertainment industry did not

00:43:14.681 --> 00:43:19.000
want .XXX TLD to be created.

00:43:19.001 --> 00:43:22.670
So, ICANN for various
reasons approved it in

00:43:22.671 --> 00:43:26.490
2005 and then the
Governmental Advisory Council,

00:43:26.491 --> 00:43:28.380
part of ICANN wrote a report

00:43:28.381 --> 00:43:31.020
saying this is a terrible
idea, don't do it.

00:43:31.021 --> 00:43:34.245
So ICANN said, "Oh, okay
fine, we won't do it".

00:43:34.246 --> 00:43:36.670
So they rejected it and

00:43:36.671 --> 00:43:39.085
then ICM Registry
filed a complaint,

00:43:39.086 --> 00:43:40.725
an independent review board

00:43:40.726 --> 00:43:42.750
upheld ICM Registry's complaint.

00:43:42.751 --> 00:43:44.160
And so, ICANN said,

00:43:44.161 --> 00:43:46.000
"Okay, I guess we have
to do it after all".

00:43:46.001 --> 00:43:48.805
And then it became
active in 2011.

00:43:48.806 --> 00:43:56.125
So, there was a story
for what XXX was for.

00:43:56.126 --> 00:43:58.580
And it represents one of

00:43:58.581 --> 00:44:02.105
these early new generic
top level domains.

00:44:02.106 --> 00:44:05.400
And so we wanted to see
what was the impact of XXX.

00:44:05.401 --> 00:44:10.100
So we would just enumerate
all the registered domains.

00:44:10.101 --> 00:44:13.040
It turns out that
because of how XXX works,

00:44:13.041 --> 00:44:14.870
it requires some inferences
not just sort of

00:44:14.871 --> 00:44:16.000
enumerating the Zone file

00:44:16.001 --> 00:44:17.605
although that's
one of the steps,

00:44:17.606 --> 00:44:19.810
crawl the content and then based

00:44:19.811 --> 00:44:22.570
upon what it is that we could
discover about that domain,

00:44:22.571 --> 00:44:25.965
we tried to infer
the registration intent

00:44:25.966 --> 00:44:27.730
of the domain in XXX.

00:44:27.731 --> 00:44:29.745
Why did that person,

00:44:29.746 --> 00:44:31.660
that individual
register that domain?

00:44:31.661 --> 00:44:34.250
And then we would also
estimate the costs and

00:44:34.251 --> 00:44:35.420
the revenue as a result of

00:44:35.421 --> 00:44:37.260
the domain registrations
to ICM Registry.

00:44:37.261 --> 00:44:40.040
How much money is ICM Registry
making as a result?

00:44:40.041 --> 00:44:43.195
Okay. The registration
process for this,

00:44:43.196 --> 00:44:45.975
winds up being
unusually complicated.

00:44:45.976 --> 00:44:47.570
There are these
things, it started

00:44:47.571 --> 00:44:48.700
with this Rollout Period,

00:44:48.701 --> 00:44:52.775
and so ICM Registry

00:44:52.776 --> 00:44:55.650
since it was a new
opening up new territory,

00:44:55.651 --> 00:44:57.660
new name territory,
it wanted to try to

00:44:57.661 --> 00:45:00.175
capitalize on new names
being available.

00:45:00.176 --> 00:45:03.275
And so it had a premium for
people to register new names.

00:45:03.276 --> 00:45:06.460
So some, one part of
that premium is for people who

00:45:06.461 --> 00:45:09.890
might want domains
in XXX at Sunrise A.

00:45:09.891 --> 00:45:11.910
And then there are people who

00:45:11.911 --> 00:45:13.985
don't want to have
domains in XXX.

00:45:13.986 --> 00:45:15.810
And so there was another period
where you could pay

00:45:15.811 --> 00:45:18.905
money so that nobody else
could grab that domain for you.

00:45:18.906 --> 00:45:23.540
So in this period Microsoft
could register Microsoft.XXX,

00:45:23.541 --> 00:45:25.465
so that nobody
else could register

00:45:25.466 --> 00:45:29.190
Microsoft.XXX for
obvious reasons.

00:45:29.191 --> 00:45:31.830
And then, there is

00:45:31.831 --> 00:45:34.415
a whole set of names
that are protected.

00:45:34.416 --> 00:45:37.370
So for example all U.S.
presidents, senators,

00:45:37.371 --> 00:45:38.785
congressmen.

00:45:38.786 --> 00:45:41.275
All of their names are
protected, can't register them.

00:45:41.276 --> 00:45:44.000
And then finally after
all this auctioning,

00:45:44.001 --> 00:45:45.260
all this crazy stuff happening,

00:45:45.261 --> 00:45:47.965
then they open it up to
sort of general registrations.

00:45:47.966 --> 00:45:50.150
And then these are,

00:45:50.151 --> 00:45:51.830
these are the prices
for registering

00:45:51.831 --> 00:45:53.890
the domain. All right.

00:45:53.891 --> 00:45:56.020
So what we wanted to do is

00:45:56.021 --> 00:45:57.935
we wanted to enumerate
all the domains of XXX,

00:45:57.936 --> 00:45:59.530
crawl them, find out and

00:45:59.531 --> 00:46:01.810
then eventually try
to infer the intent.

00:46:01.811 --> 00:46:03.830
Zone file gives you
all the domains that

00:46:03.831 --> 00:46:06.370
are registered and resolving.

00:46:06.371 --> 00:46:09.530
And then there are bunch
of names that are hidden.

00:46:09.531 --> 00:46:10.960
They're not in the Zone file.

00:46:10.961 --> 00:46:12.140
They're just hidden
because they're

00:46:12.141 --> 00:46:15.010
reserved or non-resolving,
things like that.

00:46:15.011 --> 00:46:18.700
And so we would have to sort
of infer counts and guess

00:46:18.701 --> 00:46:20.390
at what these domains
might be to

00:46:20.391 --> 00:46:23.625
try to get a sense
of the numbers there.

00:46:23.626 --> 00:46:29.700
For the domains where
we could crawl them.

00:46:29.701 --> 00:46:32.010
We downloaded
both their web content,

00:46:32.011 --> 00:46:34.450
of course all of
their DNS records

00:46:34.451 --> 00:46:36.075
as well as WHOIS records,

00:46:36.076 --> 00:46:37.690
and try to use this as a way

00:46:37.691 --> 00:46:41.350
for inferring
registration intent.

00:46:41.351 --> 00:46:46.210
So this is what happens if
you go to Microsoft.XXX.

00:46:46.211 --> 00:46:48.880
You get this page
that pops up that says

00:46:48.881 --> 00:46:50.590
this domain has been
a reserve from registration.

00:46:50.591 --> 00:46:53.150
So Microsoft paid $200 in

00:46:53.151 --> 00:46:56.850
Sunrise B so that nobody
else could register XXX.

00:46:56.851 --> 00:46:59.335
Microsoft.XXX,
including Microsoft.

00:46:59.336 --> 00:47:01.845
So Microsoft cannot
use this domain.

00:47:01.846 --> 00:47:04.890
Alright. And this web page
is served out of

00:47:04.891 --> 00:47:08.230
ICM Registry which is
a registry that owns XXX.

00:47:08.231 --> 00:47:11.670
So the intent here
is to reserve names

00:47:11.671 --> 00:47:12.820
and essentially protect you

00:47:12.821 --> 00:47:15.400
from other people
registering it.

00:47:15.401 --> 00:47:19.315
There is another way
in which sites,

00:47:19.316 --> 00:47:21.350
organizations,
people can protect

00:47:21.351 --> 00:47:23.415
themselves from other people
registering them.

00:47:23.416 --> 00:47:27.040
And it's, the categories called
Registered Non-Resolving.

00:47:27.041 --> 00:47:30.315
So for example, if
you look up UCSD.XXX.

00:47:30.316 --> 00:47:32.400
So we pay money,

00:47:32.401 --> 00:47:35.330
UCSD pays money to
ICM registry for UCSD.XXX,

00:47:35.331 --> 00:47:37.560
you get a non-existent domain.

00:47:37.561 --> 00:47:40.470
So UCSD pays money
on a yearly basis

00:47:40.471 --> 00:47:42.960
to ICM Registry so that
if anybody tries to

00:47:42.961 --> 00:47:46.150
look it up it says the domain
is non-existent and roughly

00:47:46.151 --> 00:47:49.440
speaking- so you
can't enumerate these,

00:47:49.441 --> 00:47:50.730
there's no way to enumerate
them because they're

00:47:50.731 --> 00:47:52.440
not in the Zone file
or anything like that.

00:47:52.441 --> 00:47:53.970
And so you have to try to sort

00:47:53.971 --> 00:47:55.680
of guess what their,
what their numbers are.

00:47:55.681 --> 00:47:59.210
And roughly speaking,
the amount in this category,

00:47:59.211 --> 00:48:01.340
at least in the first year
is roughly speaking

00:48:01.341 --> 00:48:02.590
the same amount in

00:48:02.591 --> 00:48:05.855
the reserved category
like Microsoft's case.

00:48:05.856 --> 00:48:08.535
And the intent here is
clearly to be defensive.

00:48:08.536 --> 00:48:09.600
You don't want somebody else

00:48:09.601 --> 00:48:10.540
registering it so you're willing

00:48:10.541 --> 00:48:12.885
to pay money so that they can't.

00:48:12.886 --> 00:48:14.070
And then there are

00:48:14.071 --> 00:48:16.355
two other categories,
one is speculative.

00:48:16.356 --> 00:48:18.360
So these are people
who are registering

00:48:18.361 --> 00:48:21.060
domains because
they believe that

00:48:21.061 --> 00:48:23.100
they'll be able to sell it at

00:48:23.101 --> 00:48:24.120
a higher price to

00:48:24.121 --> 00:48:25.915
somebody else who
wants it down the line.

00:48:25.916 --> 00:48:28.350
So parking pages, so this is

00:48:28.351 --> 00:48:30.695
an example of a parking page
of a domain in XXX.

00:48:30.696 --> 00:48:33.230
They are just, it's up there,

00:48:33.231 --> 00:48:34.790
the domain that has
been registered.

00:48:34.791 --> 00:48:36.420
It resolves, if you visit it in

00:48:36.421 --> 00:48:38.705
the browser, they just
want to sell it to you.

00:48:38.706 --> 00:48:41.075
So it's just pure speculation.

00:48:41.076 --> 00:48:43.630
And then the other is primary,

00:48:43.631 --> 00:48:45.470
this is actual content,

00:48:45.471 --> 00:48:50.740
and I didn't show a screen
shot for obvious reasons.

00:48:50.741 --> 00:48:53.930
So do the enumeration,

00:48:53.931 --> 00:48:56.340
do the crawl. Look
at everything.

00:48:56.341 --> 00:48:58.300
We know what the costs are

00:48:58.301 --> 00:48:59.830
for each of all of
the registrations,

00:48:59.831 --> 00:49:01.020
all of this kind of
stuff and then we could

00:49:01.021 --> 00:49:02.980
just multiply everything out.

00:49:02.981 --> 00:49:04.760
You get a table that
looks like this.

00:49:04.761 --> 00:49:06.880
So we have these four
categories of the intent

00:49:06.881 --> 00:49:09.305
for which people were
doing the registrations.

00:49:09.306 --> 00:49:12.325
The number of domains that
were in that category,

00:49:12.326 --> 00:49:14.400
and then multiplying
by the cost,

00:49:14.401 --> 00:49:15.690
the amount of money, the revenue

00:49:15.691 --> 00:49:18.105
that ICM Registry received.

00:49:18.106 --> 00:49:23.090
So, impressively 24 million to

00:49:23.091 --> 00:49:25.030
ICM Registry just in

00:49:25.031 --> 00:49:28.645
the initial period for
people defending their names.

00:49:28.646 --> 00:49:30.490
They are just minting money.

00:49:30.491 --> 00:49:33.145
All right.

00:49:33.146 --> 00:49:35.150
And then if you look
at the recurring

00:49:35.151 --> 00:49:36.450
costs these are the yearly,

00:49:36.451 --> 00:49:39.560
so doing the reserve in sort of

00:49:39.561 --> 00:49:40.960
the initial registration
where you pay

00:49:40.961 --> 00:49:43.595
$200 so that nobody else
could use it forever.

00:49:43.596 --> 00:49:45.320
That's just a onetime cost.

00:49:45.321 --> 00:49:48.460
But the UCSD's case where
they are doing the registry,

00:49:48.461 --> 00:49:51.310
registered non-resolving,
UCSD is paying a

00:49:51.311 --> 00:49:53.165
$100 a year to ICM Registry

00:49:53.166 --> 00:49:55.385
so that nobody else can
register that domain.

00:49:55.386 --> 00:49:58.115
And the recurring costs

00:49:58.116 --> 00:50:01.860
for the revenue that is
coming to ICM Registry,

00:50:01.861 --> 00:50:04.760
less than 10 percent of
it is due to people who

00:50:04.761 --> 00:50:09.825
actually have
adult content in XXX.

00:50:09.826 --> 00:50:13.440
So it's just a scam.
Just a big scam.

00:50:13.441 --> 00:50:16.630
All right. So, does XXX
meet the unmet needs?

00:50:16.631 --> 00:50:18.780
Absolutely not. Huge scam.

00:50:18.781 --> 00:50:20.870
What-ever adult content is

00:50:20.871 --> 00:50:22.840
in XXX or is out
on the internet.

00:50:22.841 --> 00:50:23.950
It is not in XXX.

00:50:23.951 --> 00:50:25.620
You have to go elsewhere
if you want to find it.

00:50:25.621 --> 00:50:27.535
But a huge cost
to everybody else.

00:50:27.536 --> 00:50:30.750
So clearly, a failure from

00:50:30.751 --> 00:50:32.580
the perspective of why

00:50:32.581 --> 00:50:35.915
it is that we might
introduce a new TLD.

00:50:35.916 --> 00:50:42.960
So, then we did it
for all new TLDs.

00:50:42.961 --> 00:50:45.215
So, ICANN opened up

00:50:45.216 --> 00:50:50.075
the process of registering
for new TLDs in January 2012.

00:50:50.076 --> 00:50:51.950
Anybody could go out
there and register a TLD.

00:50:51.951 --> 00:50:53.500
We were really excited and we

00:50:53.501 --> 00:50:54.510
are starting the process when we

00:50:54.511 --> 00:50:57.575
realize that there's
$200,000 cost for doing it,

00:50:57.576 --> 00:51:00.205
which is even too steep for us.

00:51:00.206 --> 00:51:02.490
But anybody can do it,

00:51:02.491 --> 00:51:03.890
there are lots of
different categories,

00:51:03.891 --> 00:51:05.810
it was very controversial
at the time.

00:51:05.811 --> 00:51:09.310
The good is that it opens
up vast new name spaces.

00:51:09.311 --> 00:51:11.925
Beyond just the crowded .com,

00:51:11.926 --> 00:51:14.200
but of course that might
introduce confusion,

00:51:14.201 --> 00:51:15.360
add cost, result in

00:51:15.361 --> 00:51:16.690
legal battles,
all this kind of stuff.

00:51:16.691 --> 00:51:18.230
So, lots of debate

00:51:18.231 --> 00:51:19.960
about whether this was
actually a good idea.

00:51:19.961 --> 00:51:21.320
So we wanted to sort of see,

00:51:21.321 --> 00:51:23.860
evaluate you know for
all of these new ones,

00:51:23.861 --> 00:51:26.480
what is going on
inside the new TLDs?

00:51:26.481 --> 00:51:28.675
Okay. What I'm going to do.

00:51:28.676 --> 00:51:30.990
So same methodology,
roughly speaking

00:51:30.991 --> 00:51:33.145
of XXX except we can't go

00:51:33.146 --> 00:51:35.035
into as much detail
because we have

00:51:35.036 --> 00:51:36.580
500 TLDs in all of

00:51:36.581 --> 00:51:37.460
the hundreds of thousands of

00:51:37.461 --> 00:51:38.720
domains that are
registered in all of them.

00:51:38.721 --> 00:51:42.170
Okay. But, I'll just

00:51:42.171 --> 00:51:43.770
sort of summarize
what the results were.

00:51:43.771 --> 00:51:48.745
So, do the new TLDs
meet unmet needs?

00:51:48.746 --> 00:51:51.490
Well, clearly there is
a demand for a new TLDs.

00:51:51.491 --> 00:51:55.820
The fact that we now have
1,200 new generic TLDs,

00:51:55.821 --> 00:51:58.855
people were really excited
about registering new TLDs.

00:51:58.856 --> 00:52:02.700
It is expensive and
we developed a sort

00:52:02.701 --> 00:52:06.745
of a profit model
for these new TLDs.

00:52:06.746 --> 00:52:08.650
How many registrations
do you need

00:52:08.651 --> 00:52:10.450
over how much time before
you become profitable?

00:52:10.451 --> 00:52:12.740
And actually it's not

00:52:12.741 --> 00:52:14.000
clear that the majority are

00:52:14.001 --> 00:52:15.550
actually going to be
profitable down the line.

00:52:15.551 --> 00:52:17.340
So, there has to be
other reasons why

00:52:17.341 --> 00:52:19.205
they're registering
these new TLDs.

00:52:19.206 --> 00:52:22.010
There was not a deluge
of sort of lawsuits or

00:52:22.011 --> 00:52:26.375
confusion over domains spanning
one TLD versus another.

00:52:26.376 --> 00:52:28.340
Certainly there are
some organizations

00:52:28.341 --> 00:52:29.380
that are protecting themselves.

00:52:29.381 --> 00:52:30.480
Microsoft would be one of them.

00:52:30.481 --> 00:52:34.805
You register Microsoft.star
and just do that.

00:52:34.806 --> 00:52:37.430
Customers are using them.

00:52:37.431 --> 00:52:39.650
So, in the top TLDs,

00:52:39.651 --> 00:52:40.810
hundreds of thousands of new

00:52:40.811 --> 00:52:42.445
domains are being registered.

00:52:42.446 --> 00:52:44.190
And, we did

00:52:44.191 --> 00:52:47.340
this machine learning analysis

00:52:47.341 --> 00:52:48.715
where we asked the question,

00:52:48.716 --> 00:52:52.110
is the content on the site
of the domain registered

00:52:52.111 --> 00:52:55.650
in the new TLD related to
the name of the new TLD?

00:52:55.651 --> 00:52:57.495
And when there is content,

00:52:57.496 --> 00:53:00.065
interestingly, 80 percent
of the time it was.

00:53:00.066 --> 00:53:01.975
So for .New York,

00:53:01.976 --> 00:53:03.665
New York related websites;

00:53:03.666 --> 00:53:05.735
.London, London
related websites.

00:53:05.736 --> 00:53:07.500
So to that extent actually,

00:53:07.501 --> 00:53:09.320
it seemed to be
working out fine.

00:53:09.321 --> 00:53:13.290
But if you sort of look
at all the registrations,

00:53:13.291 --> 00:53:14.940
it is still the case that

00:53:14.941 --> 00:53:18.920
defensive dominates
actual content.

00:53:18.921 --> 00:53:21.120
So, only 15 percent
of the domains

00:53:21.121 --> 00:53:22.870
that we were able to crawl,

00:53:22.871 --> 00:53:25.245
had real content on them.

00:53:25.246 --> 00:53:27.835
The other were either defensive,

00:53:27.836 --> 00:53:29.805
lets say, redirects or
something like that,

00:53:29.806 --> 00:53:31.035
or they were speculative.

00:53:31.036 --> 00:53:35.125
Lots of lots of parking
out there in the new TLDs.

00:53:35.126 --> 00:53:38.060
&gt;&gt; Does the 15 percent
that's new,

00:53:38.061 --> 00:53:39.250
are they replicating content

00:53:39.251 --> 00:53:40.410
that's already on the .com or

00:53:40.411 --> 00:53:43.250
something or is it
genuinely new, new, new?

00:53:43.251 --> 00:53:44.740
&gt;&gt; Generally new,
new, new. So we

00:53:44.741 --> 00:53:46.190
tried to take in account
for that if there is

00:53:46.191 --> 00:53:48.110
a redirect or if
it was similar to

00:53:48.111 --> 00:53:51.455
the .com version than
we'd say not new. Yeah.

00:53:51.456 --> 00:53:56.110
&gt;&gt; If I paid $200,000
to open the TLD,

00:53:56.111 --> 00:53:58.250
what kind of right do I have?

00:53:58.251 --> 00:54:03.160
&gt;&gt; Oh, okay. Right.
So the business model

00:54:03.161 --> 00:54:04.910
is that as a registry,

00:54:04.911 --> 00:54:09.275
you're going to own
a new TLD [inaudible] .

00:54:09.276 --> 00:54:12.030
Now you will engage in

00:54:12.031 --> 00:54:14.820
business contracts with
registrars like GoDaddy.

00:54:14.821 --> 00:54:18.095
GoDaddy will sell
domains in [inaudible] .

00:54:18.096 --> 00:54:20.290
And you get to set
what the price is.

00:54:20.291 --> 00:54:22.440
GoDaddy will take a sliver of

00:54:22.441 --> 00:54:27.390
every domain registration and
then you get the remainder.

00:54:27.391 --> 00:54:32.760
Yeah, ICANN also
gets a little too.

00:54:32.761 --> 00:54:37.400
Okay. And then, you know,

00:54:37.401 --> 00:54:38.840
this is still sort of the basis

00:54:38.841 --> 00:54:40.000
of ongoing work at looking

00:54:40.001 --> 00:54:42.660
at various kinds of abuse
but being able to do it.

00:54:42.661 --> 00:54:44.450
Now that we have all this
infrastructure being able to

00:54:44.451 --> 00:54:46.600
crawl sort of DNS at scale,

00:54:46.601 --> 00:54:50.110
of looking at abuse
in DNS at scale.

00:54:50.111 --> 00:54:52.350
&gt;&gt; So, I'm still surprised on

00:54:52.351 --> 00:54:55.075
how much new content
there seems to be.

00:54:55.076 --> 00:54:58.515
Did you compare it
against everything

00:54:58.516 --> 00:54:59.970
else or did you just compare

00:54:59.971 --> 00:55:02.200
it against things with
the same name .com?

00:55:02.201 --> 00:55:07.970
&gt;&gt; So we compared it
against the same name .com.

00:55:07.971 --> 00:55:11.470
We also, at the time
that we were

00:55:11.471 --> 00:55:13.220
crawling all of the new domains

00:55:13.221 --> 00:55:14.775
that were appearing
in the new TLDs,

00:55:14.776 --> 00:55:18.145
we also crawled
new registrations in .com,

00:55:18.146 --> 00:55:20.820
to sort of see you know what is

00:55:20.821 --> 00:55:23.165
the fraction of parked pages

00:55:23.166 --> 00:55:25.090
in the new TLDs relative to

00:55:25.091 --> 00:55:28.290
the new parked pages in
.com, things like that.

00:55:28.291 --> 00:55:30.340
And it was absolutely

00:55:30.341 --> 00:55:32.790
the case that there was
far more defensive and

00:55:32.791 --> 00:55:35.910
speculation in the new TLDs
for new registrations

00:55:35.911 --> 00:55:39.125
relative to .com,
relative to content.

00:55:39.126 --> 00:55:41.900
There was more new content
was occurring in .com at

00:55:41.901 --> 00:55:43.610
the same time that

00:55:43.611 --> 00:55:46.880
some content was
appearing in the new TLDs.

00:55:46.881 --> 00:55:51.390
And these percentages are for

00:55:51.391 --> 00:55:52.890
domains that actually had

00:55:52.891 --> 00:55:56.005
a web server respond
to an HTTP request.

00:55:56.006 --> 00:55:58.060
So there are lots of
domain, registered domains,

00:55:58.061 --> 00:56:00.550
in the new TLDs
that don't resolve,

00:56:00.551 --> 00:56:02.395
or there's some kind of error,

00:56:02.396 --> 00:56:04.215
or there's a web error
or something like that.

00:56:04.216 --> 00:56:05.850
This is just for
where we actually had

00:56:05.851 --> 00:56:09.650
content and can look at it.

00:56:10.520 --> 00:56:14.625
All right. I'll stop there.

00:56:14.626 --> 00:56:17.740
So this is still, you know,

00:56:17.741 --> 00:56:20.030
an area in which
we're very active and

00:56:20.031 --> 00:56:21.900
working not just
sort of DNS abuse

00:56:21.901 --> 00:56:23.960
but sort of current projects
we are looking at

00:56:23.961 --> 00:56:26.360
account compromised
by attackers.

00:56:26.361 --> 00:56:28.695
You paid attacker $100,

00:56:28.696 --> 00:56:30.500
you give them an email address,

00:56:30.501 --> 00:56:33.400
like victorball@microsoft.com
and then they will

00:56:33.401 --> 00:56:37.245
give you the password for
victorball@microsoft.com.

00:56:37.246 --> 00:56:40.560
Yeah. Exactly. Ad fraud,

00:56:40.561 --> 00:56:42.510
looking at security outcomes,

00:56:42.511 --> 00:56:47.185
OSN abuse, phishing, looking at,

00:56:47.186 --> 00:56:48.720
you know, VPNs are now being

00:56:48.721 --> 00:56:51.810
advertised pretty,
pretty heavily.

00:56:51.811 --> 00:56:53.800
Do VPNs actually give you

00:56:53.801 --> 00:56:55.775
the guarantees that they
advertise to give you?

00:56:55.776 --> 00:56:58.770
Are they storing
stuff and reselling,

00:56:58.771 --> 00:57:00.600
you know, interesting
questions there.

00:57:00.601 --> 00:57:03.985
&gt;&gt; Are you going to include
Facebook in that study?

00:57:03.986 --> 00:57:07.440
Because they're now
offering a VPN service

00:57:07.441 --> 00:57:08.850
It would be good for

00:57:08.851 --> 00:57:10.590
the privacy sensitive
user who doesn't

00:57:10.591 --> 00:57:12.740
want their online activity

00:57:12.741 --> 00:57:14.880
to be viewed by
a large corporation.

00:57:14.881 --> 00:57:18.250
&gt;&gt; Facebook already knows
everything about you.

00:57:18.251 --> 00:57:19.810
So if you use them as VPN,

00:57:19.811 --> 00:57:20.890
they're not going to
learn anything new.

00:57:20.891 --> 00:57:24.140
So, I mean, once you signed
up, game over already.

00:57:24.141 --> 00:57:26.425
&gt;&gt; What is lateral phishing?

00:57:26.426 --> 00:57:28.140
&gt;&gt; Lateral phising is,

00:57:28.141 --> 00:57:32.795
I'm able to phish
an account in a company.

00:57:32.796 --> 00:57:35.610
And then based upon
that phished account,

00:57:35.611 --> 00:57:40.565
then go and compromise
other accounts or other servers.

00:57:40.566 --> 00:57:43.700
So, it has become clear,

00:57:43.701 --> 00:57:45.465
so if you ever look at sort

00:57:45.466 --> 00:57:48.830
of how breaches happen
in practice so if you

00:57:48.831 --> 00:57:50.020
were to sort of let's say be

00:57:50.021 --> 00:57:51.610
a legal consultant
on a case where

00:57:51.611 --> 00:57:54.965
there are breaches
under dispute,

00:57:54.966 --> 00:57:58.015
the methodology actually is,

00:57:58.016 --> 00:58:01.555
you just get a toe-hold
anywhere in the company.

00:58:01.556 --> 00:58:03.650
And then once you get
that toe-hold then you

00:58:03.651 --> 00:58:05.460
become very clever in trying

00:58:05.461 --> 00:58:08.000
to then find
the actual resources

00:58:08.001 --> 00:58:10.290
that you're after when
you're compromising that.

00:58:10.291 --> 00:58:13.560
That's it. And in fact,

00:58:13.561 --> 00:58:16.390
it's the lateral step
that is very much

00:58:16.391 --> 00:58:18.940
understudied. That we're
trying to get a handle on.

00:58:18.941 --> 00:58:23.545
&gt;&gt; Is there a study on
online social network abuse?

00:58:23.546 --> 00:58:26.920
&gt;&gt; All right. So, this is

00:58:26.921 --> 00:58:29.360
actually a project we

00:58:29.361 --> 00:58:32.525
just submitted to using
security last week.

00:58:32.526 --> 00:58:34.440
It's completely crazy.

00:58:34.441 --> 00:58:38.615
I'm not an online
OSN user myself, but,

00:58:38.616 --> 00:58:43.660
so imagine you have

00:58:43.661 --> 00:58:46.020
an online social network
and there are

00:58:46.021 --> 00:58:47.985
various metrics about
your OSN account,

00:58:47.986 --> 00:58:49.185
how many people follow you?

00:58:49.186 --> 00:58:51.305
How many people like you?
All this kind of stuff.

00:58:51.306 --> 00:58:54.620
Okay. There are services out

00:58:54.621 --> 00:58:56.040
there that you pay

00:58:56.041 --> 00:58:58.890
them and they will
increase those metrics.

00:58:58.891 --> 00:59:04.640
And we're talking,
and these services,

00:59:05.860 --> 00:59:10.090
let's say they're making a lot

00:59:10.091 --> 00:59:12.390
of money from doing this.
A lot, a lot of money.

00:59:12.391 --> 00:59:14.075
&gt;&gt; I have a personal experience,

00:59:14.076 --> 00:59:17.540
my wife has a business,

00:59:17.541 --> 00:59:19.850
so I have this experience how

00:59:19.851 --> 00:59:21.635
they do it and how bad it is.

00:59:21.636 --> 00:59:24.070
&gt;&gt; So, the goal
of the project is

00:59:24.071 --> 00:59:28.480
to enroll in the services
using honey-pot accounts,

00:59:28.481 --> 00:59:30.555
understand how it
is that they work,

00:59:30.556 --> 00:59:33.190
based upon that understanding
create essentially

00:59:33.191 --> 00:59:34.210
signatures so you can

00:59:34.211 --> 00:59:36.350
monitor at scale for
the entire service.

00:59:36.351 --> 00:59:38.755
Look at how the service
operates at scale.

00:59:38.756 --> 00:59:40.660
Look at how much money
they're making,

00:59:40.661 --> 00:59:43.490
and then use the knowledge

00:59:43.491 --> 00:59:45.620
gained to intervene
and shut them down.

00:59:45.621 --> 00:59:48.950
And, in this case we
actually looked at

00:59:48.951 --> 00:59:50.950
different interventions
over time to see

00:59:50.951 --> 00:59:53.255
how services react to
different interventions.

00:59:53.256 --> 00:59:55.245
&gt;&gt; How about letting on,

00:59:55.246 --> 00:59:57.750
like there are services
that use these,

00:59:57.751 --> 01:00:00.090
like there are people who
use these services and

01:00:00.091 --> 01:00:02.735
so learning that these people
had used that service.

01:00:02.736 --> 01:00:05.280
&gt;&gt; We did not go down
that path but that would be

01:00:05.281 --> 01:00:07.740
another way of getting

01:00:07.741 --> 01:00:09.580
more information about
how the service works.

01:00:09.581 --> 01:00:11.760
Yes. It's just amazing

01:00:11.761 --> 01:00:15.790
that the ecosystem that
is built up around this,

01:00:15.791 --> 01:00:18.195
on metrics they don't
actually mean anything.

01:00:18.196 --> 01:00:21.930
&gt;&gt; Do you think,

01:00:21.931 --> 01:00:25.585
all these chatter around
password-less authentication,

01:00:25.586 --> 01:00:27.260
how do you see that space?

01:00:27.261 --> 01:00:28.910
And, you know going

01:00:28.911 --> 01:00:31.705
forward with passwords
still can't be repeated.

01:00:31.706 --> 01:00:33.425
Down which way,
whichever which way?

01:00:33.426 --> 01:00:34.820
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately yes.

01:00:34.821 --> 01:00:36.000
&gt;&gt; You would say, yes?

01:00:36.001 --> 01:00:39.165
&gt;&gt; I don't see passwords
going away anytime soon.

01:00:39.166 --> 01:00:41.690
&gt;&gt; Is that because
the other schemes are

01:00:41.691 --> 01:00:45.010
too clunky or very expensive?

01:00:45.011 --> 01:00:50.370
&gt;&gt; Well, it comes back again
to this usability question.

01:00:50.371 --> 01:00:51.690
Nobody wants security.

01:00:51.691 --> 01:00:52.770
Users don't want security,

01:00:52.771 --> 01:00:54.070
sites don't want security,

01:00:54.071 --> 01:00:57.315
the attackers don't want
security, nobody wants security.

01:00:57.316 --> 01:01:00.630
And because it only makes
things more of a hassle,

01:01:00.631 --> 01:01:03.910
and it's this hassle
slash convenience factor

01:01:03.911 --> 01:01:07.215
about sort of two
factor authentication

01:01:07.216 --> 01:01:09.520
and things like that
that I would say

01:01:09.521 --> 01:01:14.545
has limited the
adoption rate of it.

01:01:14.546 --> 01:01:17.890
You know, if you're
a small group

01:01:17.891 --> 01:01:20.380
creating your site for
other people to use,

01:01:20.381 --> 01:01:22.010
you just don't- it's not
where you're trying to

01:01:22.011 --> 01:01:24.485
focus all your time.

01:01:24.486 --> 01:01:28.555
Now, one way in which you
can sort of adopt it is that,

01:01:28.556 --> 01:01:30.280
you can then let

01:01:30.281 --> 01:01:34.275
Microsoft or Google or
Facebook authenticate for you.

01:01:34.276 --> 01:01:36.655
But then those companies

01:01:36.656 --> 01:01:38.820
get insight into
your site as well.

01:01:38.821 --> 01:01:43.535
So there is a little bit of
trade-off going on there.

01:01:43.536 --> 01:01:48.560
But, having the big sites
authentic for you seems like

01:01:48.561 --> 01:01:51.200
the greatest hope for

01:01:51.201 --> 01:01:52.640
small sites adopting

01:01:52.641 --> 01:01:54.975
more sophisticated
authentication techniques.

01:01:54.976 --> 01:01:59.360
They're not going to do
it themselves. But, yeah,

01:01:59.361 --> 01:02:01.045
passwords I still see them,

01:02:01.046 --> 01:02:04.840
see them here for quite a while.

01:02:05.930 --> 01:02:09.140
All right. Well,
thank you very much.

01:02:09.141 --> 01:02:10.900
I really appreciate it.

